  "before_edn": "{:db/id 17592186129268 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id 17592186128845 :question/id #uuid \"043cc93d-1eae-5640-a109-9acbe7a34f92\" :question/math-academy-id \"e-11471\" :question/problem \"If the solid region $R$ is given by \\n\\n$$\\nR = \\\\left\\\\{ (x,y,z) \\\\, : \\\\, (x-1)^2 + y^2 \\\\leq 1, \\\\: -2 \\\\leq z \\\\leq 4-y \\\\right\\\\},\\n$$\\n\\nthen express the triple integral\\n\\n$$\\n\\\\displaystyle \\\\iiint\\\\limits_R \\\\sqrt{x^2 + y^2} \\\\: \\\\textrm{d}V\\n$$\\n\\nas an equivalent integral in cylindrical polar coordinates.\" :question/worked-solution \"We will use the change of variables formula in the form\\n\\n$$\\n\\\\iiint\\\\limits_{R} f(x,y,z) \\\\, \\\\mathrm{d}V = \\n\\\\iiint\\\\limits_{\\\\Delta} \\\\, f(r \\\\cos\\\\theta, r\\\\sin\\\\theta, z) \\\\:  r \\\\: \\\\mathrm{d}z \\\\, \\\\mathrm{d}r \\\\, \\\\mathrm{d}\\\\theta.\\n$$\\n\\nIf $(r, \\\\theta, z)$ are the cylindrical polar coordinates of a point, then its Cartesian coordinates $(x,y,z)$ can be found by using the formulas\\n\\n$$\\nx = r\\\\cos\\\\theta, \\\\qquad\\ny = r\\\\sin\\\\theta, \\\\qquad \\nz = z.\\n$$\\n\\nFirst, let's sketch the projection of $R$ onto the $xy$-plane:\\n\\n![](images/a5/a551e379767395a2422f690a66fc38defab64cff03c752c0c9a5a0748561d2f7.png)\\n\\nThe region $R$ is a right vertical cylinder of radius $1$ whose axis passes through the point $(1,0)$ in the $xy$-plane, and is\\n\\n- bounded below by $z = -2,$ and\\n\\n- bounded above by $z = 4 - y = 4 - r\\\\sin{\\\\theta}.$\\n\\nWith that in mind, let's write the equation $(x-1)^2 + y^2 = 1$ in polar coordinates:\\n\\n$$\\n\\\\begin{aligned}(x-1)^{2}+{y}^{2} & =1 \\\\\\\\ {(r\\\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\\\theta -1)}^{2}+{(r\\\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\\\theta )}^{2} & =1 \\\\\\\\ {r}^{2}{cos}^{2}⁡\\\\theta -2r\\\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\\\theta +1+{r}^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡\\\\theta  & =1 \\\\\\\\ {r}^{2}({cos}^{2}⁡\\\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\\\theta )-2r\\\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\\\theta  & =0 \\\\\\\\ {r}^{2}-2r\\\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\\\theta  & =0 \\\\\\\\ r(r-2\\\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\\\theta ) & =0\\\\end{aligned}\\n$$\\n\\n \\n\\nSince $r$ cannot be identical to zero on the entire circle, we must have $r = 2\\\\cos{\\\\theta}.$\\n\\nAs a result, in cylindrical polar coordinates, our region can be expressed as \\n\\n$$\\n\\\\Delta = \\\\left\\\\{ (r,\\\\theta,z) \\\\, : \\\\, 0 \\\\leq r \\\\leq 2\\\\cos{\\\\theta}, \\\\: -\\\\dfrac{\\\\pi}{2} \\\\leq \\\\theta \\\\leq \\\\dfrac{\\\\pi}{2}, \\\\: -2 \\\\leq z \\\\leq 4-r\\\\sin{\\\\theta} \\\\right\\\\}.\\n$$\\n\\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain  \\n\\n$$\\n\\\\begin{aligned}{∭}_{R}\\\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\\\,\\\\text{d}V & ={∭}_{Δ}\\\\sqrt{(r\\\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\\\theta )^{2}+(r\\\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\\\theta )^{2}}\\\\,r\\\\,dz\\\\,dr\\\\,d\\\\theta  \\\\\\\\  & ={∭}_{Δ}\\\\sqrt{{r}^{2}({cos}^{2}⁡\\\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\\\theta )}\\\\,r\\\\,dz\\\\,dr\\\\,d\\\\theta  \\\\\\\\  & ={\\\\int }_{-\\\\pi /2}^{\\\\pi /2}{\\\\int }_{0}^{2\\\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\\\theta }{\\\\int }_{-2}^{4-r\\\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\\\theta }{r}^{2}\\\\,dz\\\\,\\\\text{d}r\\\\,\\\\text{d}\\\\theta .\\\\end{aligned}\\n$$\"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid \"aea71b56-dd09-564e-a2bf-5606233e2819\" :knowledge-point/level-coverage [{:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate}] :knowledge-point/question-generator {:db/id 17592186537081 :question-generator/id #uuid \"176ad11d-77c9-58f9-aaa7-c4d7d3927260\" :question-generator/script \"generate/scratch/17592186129268.py\"} :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id 17592186129517 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid \"267e1e75-5ebd-55ab-842b-e40b9ba9c3d5\" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value \"{\\\\int }_{\\\\pi }^{2\\\\pi }{\\\\int }_{0}^{-6\\\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\\\theta }{\\\\int }_{0}^{2+{r}^{2}}{r}^{2}\\\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\\\theta \\\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\\\theta \\\\,dz\\\\,\\\\text{d}r\\\\,\\\\text{d}\\\\theta \" :db/id 17592186555079} {:answer/id #uuid \"5c988fe0-3022-55d5-a9a9-c2db39cbe076\" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value \"{\\\\int }_{\\\\pi }^{2\\\\pi }{\\\\int }_{0}^{3}{\\\\int }_{0}^{2+r}{r}^{2}\\\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\\\theta \\\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\\\theta \\\\,dz\\\\,\\\\text{d}r\\\\,\\\\text{d}\\\\theta \" :db/id 17592186555207} {:answer/id #uuid \"a295bb42-7a73-51b6-8777-f5e3a19c436a\" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value \"{\\\\int }_{\\\\pi }^{2\\\\pi }{\\\\int }_{0}^{6\\\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\\\theta }{\\\\int }_{0}^{2+{r}^{2}}{r}^{3}\\\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\\\theta \\\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\\\theta \\\\,dz\\\\,\\\\text{d}r\\\\,\\\\text{d}\\\\theta \" :db/id 17592186555390} {:answer/id #uuid \"d507184c-2f44-5f85-9623-216dcd8ab2a5\" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value \"{\\\\int }_{\\\\pi }^{2\\\\pi }{\\\\int }_{0}^{3}{\\\\int }_{0}^{2+r}{r}^{3}\\\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\\\theta \\\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\\\theta \\\\,dz\\\\,\\\\text{d}r\\\\,\\\\text{d}\\\\theta \" :db/id 17592186555505} {:answer/id #uuid \"f1e5d412-e590-5a31-80ed-967fe6f3ff74\" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value \"{\\\\int }_{\\\\pi }^{2\\\\pi }{\\\\int }_{0}^{-6\\\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\\\theta }{\\\\int }_{0}^{2+{r}^{2}}{r}^{3}\\\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\\\theta \\\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\\\theta \\\\,dz\\\\,\\\\text{d}r\\\\,\\\\text{d}\\\\theta \" :db/id 17592186555595}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid \"f1e5d412-e590-5a31-80ed-967fe6f3ff74\" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value \"{\\\\int }_{\\\\pi }^{2\\\\pi }{\\\\int }_{0}^{-6\\\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\\\theta }{\\\\int }_{0}^{2+{r}^{2}}{r}^{3}\\\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\\\theta \\\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\\\theta \\\\,dz\\\\,\\\\text{d}r\\\\,\\\\text{d}\\\\theta \" :db/id 17592186555595} :answer-field/id #uuid \"b7d66d94-89fa-5367-8f41-89e27a901a29\" :answer-field/key \"selection\" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186126602}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid \"0593d504-dbf8-59c9-9282-2775ebdf4b88\" :question/math-academy-id \"q-161521\" :question/problem \"If the solid region $R$ is given by \\n\\n$$\\nR=\\\\{(x,y,z)\\\\,:\\\\,{x}^{2}+(y+3)^{2}\\\\le 9,\\\\,0\\\\le z\\\\le 2+{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\\\},\\n$$\\n\\nthen $$\\n\\\\displaystyle {∭}_{R}xy\\\\,\\\\text{d}V=\\n$$\" :question/worked-solution \"We will use the change of variables formula in the form\\n\\n$$\\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\\\,dV={∭}_{Δ}\\\\,f(r\\\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\\\theta ,r\\\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\\\theta ,z)\\\\,r\\\\,dz\\\\,dr\\\\,d\\\\theta .\\n$$\\n\\nIf $(r,\\\\theta ,z)$ are the cylindrical polar coordinates of a point, then its Cartesian coordinates $(x,y,z)$ can be found by using the formulas\\n\\n$$\\nx=r\\\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\\\theta ,\\\\,y=r\\\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\\\theta ,\\\\,z=z.\\n$$\\n\\nFirst, let's sketch the projection of $R$ onto the $xy$-plane:\\n\\n![](images/75/75bec31176c369e69d5b11af3eeab23f86a4f32251e37f56b225e9dd0bd4a3de.png)\\n\\nThe region $R$ is a right vertical cylinder of radius $3$ whose axis passes through the point $(0,-3)$ in the $xy$-plane, and is\\n\\n- bounded below by $z=0,$ and\\n\\n- bounded above by $z=2+{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=2+{r}^{2}.$\\n\\nWith that in mind, let's write the equation ${x}^{2}+(y+3)^{2}=9$ in polar coordinates:\\n\\n$$\\n\\\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+(y+3)^{2} & =9 \\\\\\\\ {(r\\\\operatorname{cos}\\\\theta )}^{2}+{(r\\\\operatorname{sin}\\\\theta +3)}^{2} & =9 \\\\\\\\ {r}^{2}{\\\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\\\theta +{r}^{2}{\\\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}\\\\theta +6r\\\\operatorname{sin}\\\\theta +9 & =9 \\\\\\\\ {r}^{2}({\\\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\\\theta +{\\\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}\\\\theta )+6r\\\\operatorname{sin}\\\\theta  & =0 \\\\\\\\ {r}^{2}+6r\\\\operatorname{sin}\\\\theta  & =0 \\\\\\\\ r(r+6\\\\operatorname{sin}\\\\theta ) & =0\\\\end{aligned}\\n$$\\n\\n \\n\\nSince $r$ cannot be identical to zero on the entire circle, we must have $r=-6\\\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\\\theta .$\\n\\nAs a result, in cylindrical polar coordinates, our region can be expressed as \\n\\n$$\\nΔ=\\\\{(r,\\\\theta ,z)\\\\,:\\\\,0\\\\le r\\\\le -6\\\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\\\theta ,\\\\,\\\\pi \\\\le \\\\theta \\\\le 2\\\\pi ,\\\\,0\\\\le z\\\\le 2+{r}^{2}\\\\}.\\n$$\\n\\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain  \\n\\n$$\\n\\\\begin{aligned}{∭}_{R}xy\\\\hspace{0.222em}\\\\text{d}V & ={∭}_{\\\\mathrm{Δ}}(r\\\\operatorname{cos}\\\\theta )(r\\\\operatorname{sin}\\\\theta )\\\\hspace{0.222em}r\\\\hspace{0.222em}\\\\mathrm{d}z\\\\hspace{0.167em}\\\\mathrm{d}r\\\\hspace{0.167em}\\\\mathrm{d}\\\\theta  \\\\\\\\  & ={\\\\int }_{\\\\pi }^{2\\\\pi }{\\\\int }_{0}^{\\\\bbox[Gainsboro, 2pt]{{\\\\color{blue}-6\\\\operatorname{sin}\\\\theta }}}{\\\\int }_{0}^{\\\\bbox[Gainsboro, 2pt]{{\\\\color{blue}2+{r}^{2}}}}\\\\bbox[Gainsboro, 2pt]{{\\\\color{blue}{r}^{3}\\\\operatorname{cos}\\\\theta \\\\operatorname{sin}\\\\theta }}\\\\hspace{0.222em}\\\\mathrm{d}z\\\\hspace{0.222em}\\\\text{d}r\\\\hspace{0.222em}\\\\text{d}\\\\theta .\\\\end{aligned}\\n$$\"} {:db/id 17592186129519 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid \"17bc181a-8974-5f88-9734-198cc3d71ac0\" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value \"{\\\\int }_{0}^{\\\\pi /2}{\\\\int }_{0}^{\\\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\\\theta }{\\\\int }_{1}^{2}{r}^{2}\\\\,dz\\\\,\\\\text{d}r\\\\,\\\\text{d}\\\\theta \" :db/id 17592186564976} {:answer/id #uuid \"26efd65d-e4b0-5765-a52f-7e5570d50b21\" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value \"{\\\\int }_{0}^{\\\\pi /2}{\\\\int }_{0}^{-2\\\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\\\theta }{\\\\int }_{1}^{2}{r}^{3}\\\\,dz\\\\,\\\\text{d}r\\\\,\\\\text{d}\\\\theta \" :db/id 17592186565000} {:answer/id #uuid \"3049d143-915e-5318-81f0-56825c644df0\" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value \"{\\\\int }_{0}^{\\\\pi /2}{\\\\int }_{0}^{2\\\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\\\theta }{\\\\int }_{1}^{2}{r}^{3}\\\\,dz\\\\,\\\\text{d}r\\\\,\\\\text{d}\\\\theta \" :db/id 17592186565010} {:answer/id #uuid \"7ee7a4ae-58f1-55dc-93b1-0b065f6a2752\" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value \"{\\\\int }_{0}^{\\\\pi }{\\\\int }_{0}^{2\\\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\\\theta }{\\\\int }_{1}^{2}{r}^{2}\\\\,dz\\\\,\\\\text{d}r\\\\,\\\\text{d}\\\\theta \" :db/id 17592186565131} {:answer/id #uuid \"b5c377e8-f5a2-5500-8cd7-722a46d1c396\" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value \"{\\\\int }_{0}^{\\\\pi }{\\\\int }_{0}^{-\\\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\\\theta }{\\\\int }_{1}^{2}{r}^{3}\\\\,dz\\\\,\\\\text{d}r\\\\,\\\\text{d}\\\\theta \" :db/id 17592186565211}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid \"3049d143-915e-5318-81f0-56825c644df0\" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value \"{\\\\int }_{0}^{\\\\pi /2}{\\\\int }_{0}^{2\\\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\\\theta }{\\\\int }_{1}^{2}{r}^{3}\\\\,dz\\\\,\\\\text{d}r\\\\,\\\\text{d}\\\\theta \" :db/id 17592186565010} :answer-field/id #uuid \"5de2e9ed-6a8e-5e65-b0f4-c004ab1d9bd0\" :answer-field/key \"selection\" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186126614}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid \"cc7985de-f7b8-5b62-b788-d731ea9f3ce6\" :question/math-academy-id \"q-161523\" :question/problem \"If the solid region $R$ is given by \\n\\n$$\\nR=\\\\{(x,y,z)\\\\,:\\\\,(x-1)^{2}+{y}^{2}\\\\le 1,\\\\,y\\\\ge 0,\\\\,1\\\\le z\\\\le 2\\\\},\\n$$\\n\\nthen $$\\n\\\\displaystyle {∭}_{R}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\\\,\\\\text{d}V=\\n$$\" :question/worked-solution \"We will use the change of variables formula in the form\\n\\n$$\\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\\\,dV={∭}_{Δ}\\\\,f(r\\\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\\\theta ,r\\\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\\\theta ,z)\\\\,r\\\\,dz\\\\,dr\\\\,d\\\\theta .\\n$$\\n\\nIf $(r,\\\\theta ,z)$ are the cylindrical polar coordinates of a point, then its Cartesian coordinates $(x,y,z)$ can be found by using the formulas\\n\\n$$\\nx=r\\\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\\\theta ,\\\\,y=r\\\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\\\theta ,\\\\,z=z.\\n$$\\n\\nFirst, let's sketch the projection of $R$ onto the $xy$-plane:\\n\\n![](images/60/603dc3142d8329fd9ff9fe8d09b131fe09497405f761918d59261fde314772c6.png)\\n\\nThe region $R$ is one-half of a right vertical cylinder of radius $1$ whose axis passes through the point $(1,0)$ in the $xy$-plane, and is\\n\\n- bounded below by $z=1,$ and\\n\\n- bounded above by $z=2.$\\n\\nWith that in mind, let's now write the $(x-1)^{2}+{y}^{2}=1$ in polar coordinates:\\n\\n$$\\n\\\\begin{aligned}(x-1)^{2}+{y}^{2} & =1 \\\\\\\\ (r\\\\operatorname{cos}\\\\theta -1)^{2}+(r\\\\operatorname{sin}\\\\theta )^{2} & =1 \\\\\\\\ {r}^{2}{\\\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\\\theta -2r\\\\operatorname{cos}\\\\theta +1+{r}^{2}{\\\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}\\\\theta  & =1 \\\\\\\\ {r}^{2}({\\\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\\\theta +{\\\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}\\\\theta )-2r\\\\operatorname{cos}\\\\theta  & =0 \\\\\\\\ {r}^{2}-2r\\\\operatorname{cos}\\\\theta  & =0 \\\\\\\\ r(r-2\\\\operatorname{cos}\\\\theta ) & =0\\\\end{aligned}\\n$$\\n\\n \\n\\nSince $r$ cannot be identical to zero on the entire semicircle, we must have $r=2\\\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\\\theta .$\\n\\nAs a result, in cylindrical polar coordinates, our region can be expressed as \\n\\n$$\\nΔ=\\\\{(r,\\\\theta ,z)\\\\,:\\\\,0\\\\le r\\\\le 2\\\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\\\theta ,\\\\,0\\\\le \\\\theta \\\\le \\\\frac{\\\\pi }{2},\\\\,1\\\\le z\\\\le 2\\\\}.\\n$$\\n\\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain  \\n\\n$$\\n\\\\begin{aligned}{∭}_{R}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\\\hspace{0.222em}\\\\text{d}V & ={∭}_{\\\\mathrm{Δ}}((r\\\\operatorname{cos}\\\\theta )^{2}+(r\\\\operatorname{sin}\\\\theta )^{2})\\\\hspace{0.222em}r\\\\hspace{0.222em}\\\\mathrm{d}z\\\\hspace{0.167em}\\\\mathrm{d}r\\\\hspace{0.167em}\\\\mathrm{d}\\\\theta  \\\\\\\\  & ={\\\\int }_{0}^{\\\\bbox[Gainsboro, 2pt]{{\\\\color{blue}\\\\pi /2}}}{\\\\int }_{0}^{\\\\bbox[Gainsboro, 2pt]{{\\\\color{blue}2\\\\operatorname{cos}\\\\theta }}}{\\\\int }_{1}^{2}\\\\bbox[Gainsboro, 2pt]{{\\\\color{blue}{r}^{3}}}\\\\hspace{0.222em}\\\\mathrm{d}z\\\\hspace{0.222em}\\\\text{d}r\\\\hspace{0.222em}\\\\text{d}\\\\theta .\\\\end{aligned}\\n$$\"} {:db/id 17592186367722 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid \"b265b7cc-feb9-5f37-9252-3a78cad0bd5b\" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value \"{\\\\int }_{\\\\pi /2}^{3\\\\pi /2}{\\\\int }_{0}^{\\\\bbox[Gainsboro, 2pt]{{\\\\color{blue}-6\\\\operatorname{cos}\\\\theta }}}{\\\\int }_{-8}^{\\\\bbox[Gainsboro, 2pt]{{\\\\color{blue}r\\\\operatorname{cos}\\\\theta +2}}}\\\\bbox[Gainsboro, 2pt]{{\\\\color{blue}\\\\frac{1}{\\\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+1}}}}\\\\hspace{0.222em}\\\\mathrm{d}z\\\\hspace{0.222em}\\\\text{d}r\\\\hspace{0.222em}\\\\text{d}\\\\theta\" :db/id 17592186367724} {:answer/id #uuid \"8580f07f-10cc-5678-a359-0d89f29a5582\" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value \"{\\\\int }_{\\\\pi /2}^{3\\\\pi /2}{\\\\int }_{0}^{\\\\bbox[Gainsboro, 2pt]{{\\\\color{blue}-6\\\\operatorname{sin}\\\\theta }}}{\\\\int }_{-8}^{\\\\bbox[Gainsboro, 2pt]{{\\\\color{blue}r\\\\operatorname{sin}\\\\theta +2}}}\\\\bbox[Gainsboro, 2pt]{{\\\\color{blue}\\\\frac{1}{\\\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+1}}}}\\\\hspace{0.222em}\\\\mathrm{d}z\\\\hspace{0.222em}\\\\text{d}r\\\\hspace{0.222em}\\\\text{d}\\\\theta\" :db/id 17592186367725} {:answer/id #uuid \"5d8af12c-fb84-5a5a-a7a2-521fb178be84\" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value \"{\\\\int }_{\\\\pi /2}^{3\\\\pi /2}{\\\\int }_{0}^{\\\\bbox[Gainsboro, 2pt]{{\\\\color{blue}-6\\\\operatorname{sin}\\\\theta }}}{\\\\int }_{-8}^{\\\\bbox[Gainsboro, 2pt]{{\\\\color{blue}r\\\\operatorname{cos}\\\\theta +2}}}\\\\bbox[Gainsboro, 2pt]{{\\\\color{blue}\\\\frac{r}{\\\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+1}}}}\\\\hspace{0.222em}\\\\mathrm{d}z\\\\hspace{0.222em}\\\\text{d}r\\\\hspace{0.222em}\\\\text{d}\\\\theta\" :db/id 17592186367726} {:answer/id #uuid \"54950957-60fb-56ff-96f5-e10b21a3d9ca\" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value \"{\\\\int }_{\\\\pi /2}^{3\\\\pi /2}{\\\\int }_{0}^{\\\\bbox[Gainsboro, 2pt]{{\\\\color{blue}-6\\\\operatorname{cos}\\\\theta }}}{\\\\int }_{-8}^{\\\\bbox[Gainsboro, 2pt]{{\\\\color{blue}r\\\\operatorname{sin}\\\\theta +2}}}\\\\bbox[Gainsboro, 2pt]{{\\\\color{blue}\\\\frac{r}{\\\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+1}}}}\\\\hspace{0.222em}\\\\mathrm{d}z\\\\hspace{0.222em}\\\\text{d}r\\\\hspace{0.222em}\\\\text{d}\\\\theta\" :db/id 17592186367727} {:answer/id #uuid \"dff4f68f-af71-5932-8072-ae940cffd5e2\" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value \"{\\\\int }_{\\\\pi /2}^{3\\\\pi /2}{\\\\int }_{0}^{\\\\bbox[Gainsboro, 2pt]{{\\\\color{blue}-6\\\\operatorname{cos}\\\\theta }}}{\\\\int }_{-8}^{\\\\bbox[Gainsboro, 2pt]{{\\\\color{blue}r\\\\operatorname{cos}\\\\theta +2}}}\\\\bbox[Gainsboro, 2pt]{{\\\\color{blue}\\\\frac{r}{\\\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+1}}}}\\\\hspace{0.222em}\\\\mathrm{d}z\\\\hspace{0.222em}\\\\text{d}r\\\\hspace{0.222em}\\\\text{d}\\\\theta\" :db/id 17592186367728}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid \"dff4f68f-af71-5932-8072-ae940cffd5e2\" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value \"{\\\\int }_{\\\\pi /2}^{3\\\\pi /2}{\\\\int }_{0}^{\\\\bbox[Gainsboro, 2pt]{{\\\\color{blue}-6\\\\operatorname{cos}\\\\theta }}}{\\\\int }_{-8}^{\\\\bbox[Gainsboro, 2pt]{{\\\\color{blue}r\\\\operatorname{cos}\\\\theta +2}}}\\\\bbox[Gainsboro, 2pt]{{\\\\color{blue}\\\\frac{r}{\\\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+1}}}}\\\\hspace{0.222em}\\\\mathrm{d}z\\\\hspace{0.222em}\\\\text{d}r\\\\hspace{0.222em}\\\\text{d}\\\\theta\" :db/id 17592186367728} :answer-field/id #uuid \"8751e409-d7fb-5aec-8818-db14c330ee72\" :answer-field/key \"selection\" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186367723}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid \"f13fe1ec-6a87-5577-82ff-9825a15656c9\" :question/math-academy-id \"q-161630\" :question/problem \"If the solid region $R$ is given by \\n\\n$$\\nR=\\\\{(x,y,z)\\\\,:\\\\,(x+3)^{2}+{y}^{2}\\\\le 9,\\\\,-8\\\\le z\\\\le x+2\\\\},\\n$$\\n\\nthen $$\\n\\\\displaystyle {∭}_{R}\\\\frac{1}{\\\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+1}}\\\\,\\\\text{d}V=\\n$$\" :question/worked-solution \"We will use the change of variables formula in the form\\n\\n$$\\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\\\,dV={∭}_{Δ}\\\\,f(r\\\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\\\theta ,r\\\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\\\theta ,z)\\\\,r\\\\,dz\\\\,dr\\\\,d\\\\theta .\\n$$\\n\\nIf $(r,\\\\theta ,z)$ are the cylindrical polar coordinates of a point, then its Cartesian coordinates $(x,y,z)$ can be found by using the formulas\\n\\n$$\\nx=r\\\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\\\theta ,\\\\,y=r\\\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\\\theta ,\\\\,z=z.\\n$$\\n\\nFirst, let's sketch the projection of $R$ onto the $xy$-plane:\\n\\n![](images/3c/3c03dde21942c60de527ace896bb51ad8070781126d72f5be5e2f6069700736d.png)\\n\\nThe region $R$ is a right vertical cylinder of radius $3$ whose axis passes through the point $(-3,0)$ in the $xy$-plane, and is\\n\\n- bounded below by $z=-8,$ and\\n\\n- bounded above by $z=x+2=r\\\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\\\theta +2.$\\n\\nWith that in mind, let's write the equation $(x+3)^{2}+{y}^{2}=9$ in polar coordinates:\\n\\n$$\\n\\\\begin{aligned}(x+3)^{2}+{y}^{2} & =9 \\\\\\\\ {(r\\\\operatorname{cos}\\\\theta +3)}^{2}+{(r\\\\operatorname{sin}\\\\theta )}^{2} & =9 \\\\\\\\ {r}^{2}{\\\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\\\theta +6r\\\\operatorname{cos}\\\\theta +9+{r}^{2}{\\\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}\\\\theta  & =9 \\\\\\\\ {r}^{2}({\\\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\\\theta +{\\\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}\\\\theta )+6r\\\\operatorname{cos}\\\\theta  & =0 \\\\\\\\ {r}^{2}+6r\\\\operatorname{cos}\\\\theta  & =0 \\\\\\\\ r(r+6\\\\operatorname{cos}\\\\theta ) & =0\\\\end{aligned}\\n$$\\n\\n \\n\\nSince $r$ cannot be identical to zero on the entire circle, we must have $r=-6\\\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\\\theta .$\\n\\nAs a result, in cylindrical polar coordinates, our region can be expressed as \\n\\n$$\\nΔ=\\\\{(r,\\\\theta ,z)\\\\,:\\\\,0\\\\le r\\\\le -6\\\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\\\theta ,\\\\,\\\\frac{\\\\pi }{2}\\\\le \\\\theta \\\\le \\\\frac{3\\\\pi }{2},\\\\,-8\\\\le z\\\\le r\\\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\\\theta +2\\\\}.\\n$$\\n\\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain  \\n\\n$$\\n\\\\begin{aligned}{∭}_{R}\\\\frac{1}{\\\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+1}}\\\\hspace{0.222em}\\\\text{d}V & ={∭}_{\\\\mathrm{Δ}}\\\\frac{1}{\\\\sqrt{(r\\\\operatorname{cos}\\\\theta )^{2}+(r\\\\operatorname{sin}\\\\theta )^{2}+1}}\\\\cdot r\\\\hspace{0.222em}\\\\mathrm{d}z\\\\hspace{0.167em}\\\\mathrm{d}r\\\\hspace{0.167em}\\\\mathrm{d}\\\\theta  \\\\\\\\  & ={∭}_{\\\\mathrm{Δ}}\\\\frac{r}{\\\\sqrt{{r}^{2}({\\\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\\\theta +{\\\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}\\\\theta )+1}}\\\\hspace{0.222em}\\\\mathrm{d}z\\\\hspace{0.167em}\\\\mathrm{d}r\\\\hspace{0.167em}\\\\mathrm{d}\\\\theta  \\\\\\\\  & ={\\\\int }_{\\\\pi /2}^{3\\\\pi /2}{\\\\int }_{0}^{\\\\bbox[Gainsboro, 2pt]{{\\\\color{blue}-6\\\\operatorname{cos}\\\\theta }}}{\\\\int }_{-8}^{\\\\bbox[Gainsboro, 2pt]{{\\\\color{blue}r\\\\operatorname{cos}\\\\theta +2}}}\\\\bbox[Gainsboro, 2pt]{{\\\\color{blue}\\\\frac{r}{\\\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+1}}}}\\\\hspace{0.222em}\\\\mathrm{d}z\\\\hspace{0.222em}\\\\text{d}r\\\\hspace{0.222em}\\\\text{d}\\\\theta .\\\\end{aligned}\\n$$\"} {:db/id 17592186367729 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid \"19b100d2-4636-55d3-9270-cc48d2b6fc52\" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value \"{\\\\int }_{3\\\\pi /2}^{2\\\\pi }{\\\\int }_{0}^{\\\\bbox[Gainsboro, 2pt]{{\\\\color{blue}6\\\\operatorname{cos}\\\\theta }}}{\\\\int }_{\\\\bbox[Gainsboro, 2pt]{{\\\\color{blue}r\\\\operatorname{sin}\\\\theta }}}^{6}\\\\bbox[Gainsboro, 2pt]{{\\\\color{blue}\\\\frac{r}{\\\\sqrt{z+7}}}}\\\\hspace{0.222em}\\\\mathrm{d}z\\\\hspace{0.222em}\\\\text{d}r\\\\hspace{0.222em}\\\\text{d}\\\\theta\" :db/id 17592186367731} {:answer/id #uuid \"9a29a4a1-8401-5c26-985a-c9e53ed3d39d\" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value \"{\\\\int }_{3\\\\pi /2}^{2\\\\pi }{\\\\int }_{0}^{\\\\bbox[Gainsboro, 2pt]{{\\\\color{blue}6\\\\operatorname{cos}\\\\theta }}}{\\\\int }_{\\\\bbox[Gainsboro, 2pt]{{\\\\color{blue}r\\\\operatorname{sin}\\\\theta }}}^{6}\\\\bbox[Gainsboro, 2pt]{{\\\\color{blue}\\\\frac{1}{\\\\sqrt{z+7}}}}\\\\hspace{0.222em}\\\\mathrm{d}z\\\\hspace{0.222em}\\\\text{d}r\\\\hspace{0.222em}\\\\text{d}\\\\theta\" :db/id 17592186367732} {:answer/id #uuid \"cfe69c16-aea5-5c84-b3e2-6e91f9235a94\" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value \"{\\\\int }_{3\\\\pi /2}^{2\\\\pi }{\\\\int }_{0}^{\\\\bbox[Gainsboro, 2pt]{{\\\\color{blue}-6\\\\operatorname{sin}\\\\theta }}}{\\\\int }_{\\\\bbox[Gainsboro, 2pt]{{\\\\color{blue}r\\\\operatorname{sin}\\\\theta }}}^{6}\\\\bbox[Gainsboro, 2pt]{{\\\\color{blue}\\\\frac{1}{\\\\sqrt{z+7}}}}\\\\hspace{0.222em}\\\\mathrm{d}z\\\\hspace{0.222em}\\\\text{d}r\\\\hspace{0.222em}\\\\text{d}\\\\theta\" :db/id 17592186367733} {:answer/id #uuid \"6d67683a-c100-5cd2-85f2-e42c45195bdc\" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value \"{\\\\int }_{3\\\\pi /2}^{2\\\\pi }{\\\\int }_{0}^{\\\\bbox[Gainsboro, 2pt]{{\\\\color{blue}6\\\\operatorname{cos}\\\\theta }}}{\\\\int }_{\\\\bbox[Gainsboro, 2pt]{{\\\\color{blue}r\\\\operatorname{cos}\\\\theta }}}^{6}\\\\bbox[Gainsboro, 2pt]{{\\\\color{blue}\\\\frac{r}{\\\\sqrt{z+7}}}}\\\\hspace{0.222em}\\\\mathrm{d}z\\\\hspace{0.222em}\\\\text{d}r\\\\hspace{0.222em}\\\\text{d}\\\\theta\" :db/id 17592186367734} {:answer/id #uuid \"30a2338f-aa3a-5e0e-a5d0-96cd484338d9\" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value \"{\\\\int }_{3\\\\pi /2}^{2\\\\pi }{\\\\int }_{0}^{\\\\bbox[Gainsboro, 2pt]{{\\\\color{blue}-6\\\\operatorname{sin}\\\\theta }}}{\\\\int }_{\\\\bbox[Gainsboro, 2pt]{{\\\\color{blue}r\\\\operatorname{cos}\\\\theta }}}^{6}\\\\bbox[Gainsboro, 2pt]{{\\\\color{blue}\\\\frac{r}{\\\\sqrt{z+7}}}}\\\\hspace{0.222em}\\\\mathrm{d}z\\\\hspace{0.222em}\\\\text{d}r\\\\hspace{0.222em}\\\\text{d}\\\\theta\" :db/id 17592186367735}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid \"19b100d2-4636-55d3-9270-cc48d2b6fc52\" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value \"{\\\\int }_{3\\\\pi /2}^{2\\\\pi }{\\\\int }_{0}^{\\\\bbox[Gainsboro, 2pt]{{\\\\color{blue}6\\\\operatorname{cos}\\\\theta }}}{\\\\int }_{\\\\bbox[Gainsboro, 2pt]{{\\\\color{blue}r\\\\operatorname{sin}\\\\theta }}}^{6}\\\\bbox[Gainsboro, 2pt]{{\\\\color{blue}\\\\frac{r}{\\\\sqrt{z+7}}}}\\\\hspace{0.222em}\\\\mathrm{d}z\\\\hspace{0.222em}\\\\text{d}r\\\\hspace{0.222em}\\\\text{d}\\\\theta\" :db/id 17592186367731} :answer-field/id #uuid \"29402b40-ddfd-5d71-9239-ec1aa69d5ac0\" :answer-field/key \"selection\" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186367730}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid \"d873cc92-450d-547b-9f0c-590b7060a8cb\" :question/math-academy-id \"q-161636\" :question/problem \"If the solid region $R$ is given by \\n\\n$$\\nR=\\\\{(x,y,z)\\\\hspace{0.167em}:\\\\hspace{0.167em}(x-3)^{2}+{y}^{2}\\\\le 9,\\\\hspace{0.222em}y\\\\le 0,\\\\hspace{0.222em}y\\\\le z\\\\le 6\\\\},\\n$$\\n\\nthen $$\\n\\\\displaystyle {∭}_{R}\\\\frac{1}{\\\\sqrt{z+7}}\\\\,\\\\text{d}V=\\n$$\" :question/worked-solution \"We will use the change of variables formula in the form\\n\\n$$\\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\\\,dV={∭}_{Δ}\\\\,f(r\\\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\\\theta ,r\\\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\\\theta ,z)\\\\,r\\\\,dz\\\\,dr\\\\,d\\\\theta .\\n$$\\n\\nIf $(r,\\\\theta ,z)$ are the cylindrical polar coordinates of a point, then its Cartesian coordinates $(x,y,z)$ can be found by using the formulas\\n\\n$$\\nx=r\\\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\\\theta ,\\\\,y=r\\\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\\\theta ,\\\\,z=z.\\n$$\\n\\nFirst, let's sketch the projection of $R$ onto the $xy$-plane:\\n\\n![](images/cc/ccc5cab37aed89c0d701ce0195414796211ddc73a498eaaa1ff145a45c42e9fd.png)\\n\\nThe region $R$ is one-half of a right vertical cylinder of radius $3$ whose axis passes through the point $(3,0)$ in the $xy$-plane, and is\\n\\n- bounded below by $z=y=r\\\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\\\theta ,$ and\\n\\n- bounded above by $z=6.$\\n\\nWith that in mind, let's now write the $(x-3)^{2}+{y}^{2}=9$ in polar coordinates:\\n\\n$$\\n\\\\begin{aligned}(x-3)^{2}+{y}^{2} & =9 \\\\\\\\ (r\\\\operatorname{cos}\\\\theta -3)^{2}+(r\\\\operatorname{sin}\\\\theta )^{2} & =9 \\\\\\\\ {r}^{2}{\\\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\\\theta -6r\\\\operatorname{cos}\\\\theta +9+{r}^{2}{\\\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}\\\\theta  & =9 \\\\\\\\ {r}^{2}({\\\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\\\theta +{\\\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}\\\\theta )-6r\\\\operatorname{cos}\\\\theta  & =0 \\\\\\\\ {r}^{2}-6r\\\\operatorname{cos}\\\\theta  & =0 \\\\\\\\ r(r-6\\\\operatorname{cos}\\\\theta ) & =0\\\\end{aligned}\\n$$\\n\\n \\n\\nSince $r$ cannot be identical to zero on the entire semicircle, we must have $r=6\\\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\\\theta .$\\n\\nAs a result, in cylindrical polar coordinates, our region can be expressed as \\n\\n$$\\n\\\\mathrm{Δ}=\\\\{(r,\\\\theta ,z)\\\\hspace{0.167em}:\\\\hspace{0.167em}0\\\\le r\\\\le 6\\\\operatorname{cos}\\\\theta ,\\\\hspace{0.222em}\\\\frac{3\\\\pi }{2}\\\\le \\\\theta \\\\le 2\\\\pi ,\\\\hspace{0.222em}r\\\\operatorname{sin}\\\\theta \\\\le z\\\\le 6\\\\}.\\n$$\\n\\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain  \\n\\n$$\\n\\\\begin{aligned}{∭}_{R}\\\\frac{1}{\\\\sqrt{z+7}}\\\\hspace{0.222em}\\\\text{d}V & ={∭}_{\\\\mathrm{Δ}}\\\\frac{1}{\\\\sqrt{z+7}}\\\\cdot r\\\\hspace{0.222em}\\\\mathrm{d}z\\\\hspace{0.167em}\\\\mathrm{d}r\\\\hspace{0.167em}\\\\mathrm{d}\\\\theta  \\\\\\\\  & ={\\\\int }_{3\\\\pi /2}^{2\\\\pi }{\\\\int }_{0}^{\\\\bbox[Gainsboro, 2pt]{{\\\\color{blue}6\\\\operatorname{cos}\\\\theta }}}{\\\\int }_{\\\\bbox[Gainsboro, 2pt]{{\\\\color{blue}r\\\\operatorname{sin}\\\\theta }}}^{6}\\\\bbox[Gainsboro, 2pt]{{\\\\color{blue}\\\\frac{r}{\\\\sqrt{z+7}}}}\\\\hspace{0.222em}\\\\mathrm{d}z\\\\hspace{0.222em}\\\\text{d}r\\\\hspace{0.222em}\\\\text{d}\\\\theta .\\\\end{aligned}\\n$$\"} {:db/id 17592186427905 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid \"07316687-6f83-5fed-b3ec-6bddf779b76b\" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value \"{\\\\int }_{\\\\pi /2}^{\\\\pi }{\\\\int }_{0}^{4\\\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\\\theta }{\\\\int }_{-2}^{\\\\sqrt{16-{r}^{2}}}{r}^{2}\\\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\\\theta \\\\,dz\\\\,\\\\text{d}r\\\\,\\\\text{d}\\\\theta \" :db/id 17592186427865} {:answer/id #uuid \"8a070aa5-47af-5201-85a2-9449ca643060\" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value \"{\\\\int }_{\\\\pi /2}^{\\\\pi }{\\\\int }_{0}^{4\\\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\\\theta }{\\\\int }_{-2}^{\\\\sqrt{16-{r}^{2}}}{r}^{3}\\\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\\\theta \\\\,dz\\\\,\\\\text{d}r\\\\,\\\\text{d}\\\\theta \" :db/id 17592186427880} {:answer/id #uuid \"aa6ce7d9-8ba5-51ed-8961-057d4fc31ae0\" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value \"{\\\\int }_{\\\\pi /2}^{3\\\\pi /2}{\\\\int }_{0}^{-4\\\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\\\theta }{\\\\int }_{-2}^{\\\\sqrt{16-{r}^{2}}}{r}^{2}\\\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\\\theta \\\\,dz\\\\,\\\\text{d}r\\\\,\\\\text{d}\\\\theta \" :db/id 17592186427884} {:answer/id #uuid \"b00512c6-580b-5ace-be32-2aca9c459007\" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value \"{\\\\int }_{\\\\pi /2}^{3\\\\pi /2}{\\\\int }_{0}^{4\\\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\\\theta }{\\\\int }_{-2}^{\\\\sqrt{16-{r}^{2}}}{r}^{3}\\\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\\\theta \\\\,dz\\\\,\\\\text{d}r\\\\,\\\\text{d}\\\\theta \" :db/id 17592186427885} {:answer/id #uuid \"c7c92edf-23c0-5c0c-b7bb-0f570aaf2732\" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value \"{\\\\int }_{\\\\pi /2}^{\\\\pi }{\\\\int }_{0}^{-4\\\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\\\theta }{\\\\int }_{-2}^{\\\\sqrt{16-{r}^{2}}}{r}^{3}\\\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\\\theta \\\\,dz\\\\,\\\\text{d}r\\\\,\\\\text{d}\\\\theta \" :db/id 17592186427887}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid \"8a070aa5-47af-5201-85a2-9449ca643060\" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value \"{\\\\int }_{\\\\pi /2}^{\\\\pi }{\\\\int }_{0}^{4\\\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\\\theta }{\\\\int }_{-2}^{\\\\sqrt{16-{r}^{2}}}{r}^{3}\\\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\\\theta \\\\,dz\\\\,\\\\text{d}r\\\\,\\\\text{d}\\\\theta \" :db/id 17592186427880} :answer-field/id #uuid \"5a4c28d8-7673-5151-abc1-64dbe75c6bbb\" :answer-field/key \"selection\" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186427898}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid \"f86783a2-30c3-5d18-9eb0-dc2efdbbae6a\" :question/math-academy-id \"q-161633\" :question/problem \"If the solid region $R$ is given by \\n\\n$$\\nR=\\\\{(x,y,z)\\\\,:\\\\,{x}^{2}+(y-2)^{2}\\\\le 4,\\\\,x\\\\le 0,\\\\,-2\\\\le z\\\\le \\\\sqrt{16-({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})}\\\\},\\n$$\\n\\nthen $$\\n\\\\displaystyle {∭}_{R}2xy\\\\,\\\\text{d}V=\\n$$\" :question/worked-solution \"We will use the change of variables formula in the form\\n\\n$$\\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\\\,dV={∭}_{Δ}\\\\,f(r\\\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\\\theta ,r\\\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\\\theta ,z)\\\\,r\\\\,dz\\\\,dr\\\\,d\\\\theta .\\n$$\\n\\nIf $(r,\\\\theta ,z)$ are the cylindrical polar coordinates of a point, then its Cartesian coordinates $(x,y,z)$ can be found by using the formulas\\n\\n$$\\nx=r\\\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\\\theta ,\\\\,y=r\\\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\\\theta ,\\\\,z=z.\\n$$\\n\\nFirst, let's sketch the projection of $R$ onto the $xy$-plane:\\n\\n![](images/83/831de8cf1d9833f8674a7e4cb69d01098970eefb9cf576c0537fa7be471c8c2c.png)\\n\\nThe region $R$ is one-half of a right vertical cylinder of radius $2$ whose axis passes through the point $(0,2)$ in the $xy$-plane, and is\\n\\n- bounded below by $z=-2,$ and\\n\\n- bounded above by $z=\\\\sqrt{16-({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})}=\\\\sqrt{16-{r}^{2}}.$\\n\\nWith that in mind, let's now write the ${x}^{2}+(y-2)^{2}=4$ in polar coordinates:\\n\\n$$\\n\\\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+(y-2)^{2} & =4 \\\\\\\\ (r\\\\operatorname{cos}\\\\theta )^{2}+(r\\\\operatorname{sin}\\\\theta -2)^{2} & =4 \\\\\\\\ {r}^{2}{\\\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\\\theta +{r}^{2}{\\\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}\\\\theta -4r\\\\operatorname{sin}\\\\theta +4 & =4 \\\\\\\\ {r}^{2}({\\\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\\\theta +{\\\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}\\\\theta )-4r\\\\operatorname{sin}\\\\theta  & =0 \\\\\\\\ {r}^{2}-4r\\\\operatorname{sin}\\\\theta  & =0 \\\\\\\\ r(r-4\\\\operatorname{sin}\\\\theta ) & =0\\\\end{aligned}\\n$$\\n\\n \\n\\nSince $r$ cannot be identical to zero on the entire semicircle, we must have $r=4\\\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\\\theta .$\\n\\nAs a result, in cylindrical polar coordinates, our region can be expressed as \\n\\n$$\\nΔ=\\\\{(r,\\\\theta ,z)\\\\,:\\\\,0\\\\le r\\\\le 4\\\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\\\theta ,\\\\,\\\\frac{\\\\pi }{2}\\\\le \\\\theta \\\\le \\\\pi ,\\\\,-2\\\\le z\\\\le \\\\sqrt{16-{r}^{2}}\\\\}.\\n$$\\n\\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain\\n\\n$$\\n\\\\begin{aligned}{∭}_{R}2xy\\\\hspace{0.222em}\\\\text{d}V & ={∭}_{\\\\mathrm{Δ}}2(r\\\\operatorname{cos}\\\\theta )(r\\\\operatorname{sin}\\\\theta )\\\\cdot r\\\\hspace{0.222em}\\\\mathrm{d}z\\\\hspace{0.167em}\\\\mathrm{d}r\\\\hspace{0.167em}\\\\mathrm{d}\\\\theta  \\\\\\\\  & ={\\\\int }_{\\\\pi /2}^{\\\\bbox[Gainsboro, 2pt]{{\\\\color{blue}\\\\pi }}}{\\\\int }_{0}^{\\\\bbox[Gainsboro, 2pt]{{\\\\color{blue}4\\\\operatorname{sin}\\\\theta }}}{\\\\int }_{-2}^{\\\\sqrt{16-{r}^{2}}}\\\\bbox[Gainsboro, 2pt]{{\\\\color{blue}{r}^{3}\\\\operatorname{sin}2\\\\theta }}\\\\hspace{0.222em}\\\\mathrm{d}z\\\\hspace{0.222em}\\\\text{d}r\\\\hspace{0.222em}\\\\text{d}\\\\theta .\\\\end{aligned}\\n$$\"} {:db/id 17592186427906 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid \"6caf10ce-b939-51d0-8ac6-b9727918700b\" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value \"{\\\\int }_{0}^{\\\\pi }{\\\\int }_{0}^{8\\\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\\\theta }{\\\\int }_{-5}^{r\\\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\\\theta }\\\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+1}\\\\,dz\\\\,\\\\text{d}r\\\\,\\\\text{d}\\\\theta \" :db/id 17592186427874} {:answer/id #uuid \"88813988-bed1-58d5-9361-f7b9c3b921af\" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value \"{\\\\int }_{0}^{\\\\pi /2}{\\\\int }_{0}^{8\\\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\\\theta }{\\\\int }_{-5}^{r\\\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\\\theta }r\\\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+1}\\\\,dz\\\\,\\\\text{d}r\\\\,\\\\text{d}\\\\theta \" :db/id 17592186427879} {:answer/id #uuid \"aa5a0734-d3ec-5220-8c59-ae2d980b4839\" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value \"{\\\\int }_{0}^{\\\\pi /2}{\\\\int }_{0}^{8\\\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\\\theta }{\\\\int }_{-5}^{r\\\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\\\theta }r\\\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+1}\\\\,dz\\\\,\\\\text{d}r\\\\,\\\\text{d}\\\\theta \" :db/id 17592186427883} {:answer/id #uuid \"d4e25a86-fc0b-500c-9ea6-617a19745775\" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value \"{\\\\int }_{0}^{\\\\pi /2}{\\\\int }_{0}^{8\\\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\\\theta }{\\\\int }_{-5}^{r\\\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\\\theta }\\\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+1}\\\\,dz\\\\,\\\\text{d}r\\\\,\\\\text{d}\\\\theta \" :db/id 17592186427888} {:answer/id #uuid \"f1e27cb0-5d40-54cb-b1f4-f82c347f9fc7\" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value \"{\\\\int }_{0}^{\\\\pi }{\\\\int }_{0}^{8\\\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\\\theta }{\\\\int }_{-5}^{r\\\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\\\theta }r\\\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+1}\\\\,dz\\\\,\\\\text{d}r\\\\,\\\\text{d}\\\\theta \" :db/id 17592186427891}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid \"88813988-bed1-58d5-9361-f7b9c3b921af\" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value \"{\\\\int }_{0}^{\\\\pi /2}{\\\\int }_{0}^{8\\\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\\\theta }{\\\\int }_{-5}^{r\\\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\\\theta }r\\\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+1}\\\\,dz\\\\,\\\\text{d}r\\\\,\\\\text{d}\\\\theta \" :db/id 17592186427879} :answer-field/id #uuid \"ae592001-7c80-5683-9d64-6ebfa88e4b3c\" :answer-field/key \"selection\" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186427899}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid \"fa2c3789-4a9d-5deb-a378-eba9cd152cf2\" :question/math-academy-id \"q-161637\" :question/problem \"If the solid region $R$ is given by \\n\\n$$\\nR=\\\\{(x,y,z)\\\\,:\\\\,{x}^{2}+(y-4)^{2}\\\\le 16,\\\\,x\\\\ge 0,\\\\,-5\\\\le z\\\\le x\\\\},\\n$$\\n\\nthen $$\\n\\\\displaystyle {∭}_{R}\\\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+1}\\\\,\\\\text{d}V=\\n$$\" :question/worked-solution \"We will use the change of variables formula in the form\\n\\n$$\\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\\\,dV={∭}_{Δ}\\\\,f(r\\\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\\\theta ,r\\\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\\\theta ,z)\\\\,r\\\\,dz\\\\,dr\\\\,d\\\\theta .\\n$$\\n\\nIf $(r,\\\\theta ,z)$ are the cylindrical polar coordinates of a point, then its Cartesian coordinates $(x,y,z)$ can be found by using the formulas\\n\\n$$\\nx=r\\\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\\\theta ,\\\\,y=r\\\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\\\theta ,\\\\,z=z.\\n$$\\n\\nFirst, let's sketch the projection of $R$ onto the $xy$-plane:\\n\\n![](images/b6/b66b8db18b2f5773addc2d00c02f2f15f4a7ead53323418e63bd50457f74466b.png)\\n\\nThe region $R$ is one-half of a right vertical cylinder of radius $4$ whose axis passes through the point $(0,4)$ in the $xy$-plane, and is\\n\\n- bounded below by $z=-5,$ and\\n\\n- bounded above by $z=x=r\\\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\\\theta .$\\n\\nWith that in mind, let's now write the ${x}^{2}+(y-4)^{2}=16$ in polar coordinates:\\n\\n$$\\n\\\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+(y-4)^{2} & =16 \\\\\\\\ (r\\\\operatorname{cos}\\\\theta )^{2}+(r\\\\operatorname{sin}\\\\theta -4)^{2} & =16 \\\\\\\\ {r}^{2}{\\\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\\\theta +{r}^{2}{\\\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}\\\\theta -8r\\\\operatorname{sin}\\\\theta +16 & =16 \\\\\\\\ {r}^{2}({\\\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\\\theta +{\\\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}\\\\theta )-8r\\\\operatorname{sin}\\\\theta  & =0 \\\\\\\\ {r}^{2}-8r\\\\operatorname{sin}\\\\theta  & =0 \\\\\\\\ r(r-8\\\\operatorname{sin}\\\\theta ) & =0\\\\end{aligned}\\n$$\\n\\n \\n\\nSince $r$ cannot be identical to zero on the entire semicircle, we must have $r=8\\\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\\\theta .$\\n\\nAs a result, in cylindrical polar coordinates, our region can be expressed as \\n\\n$$\\nΔ=\\\\{(r,\\\\theta ,z)\\\\,:\\\\,0\\\\le r\\\\le 8\\\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\\\theta ,\\\\,0\\\\le \\\\theta \\\\le \\\\frac{\\\\pi }{2},\\\\,-5\\\\le z\\\\le r\\\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\\\theta \\\\}.\\n$$\\n\\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain\\n\\n$$\\n\\\\begin{aligned}{∭}_{R}\\\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+1}\\\\hspace{0.222em}\\\\text{d}V & ={∭}_{\\\\mathrm{Δ}}\\\\sqrt{(r\\\\operatorname{cos}\\\\theta )^{2}+(r\\\\operatorname{sin}\\\\theta )^{2}+1}\\\\cdot r\\\\hspace{0.222em}\\\\mathrm{d}z\\\\hspace{0.167em}\\\\mathrm{d}r\\\\hspace{0.167em}\\\\mathrm{d}\\\\theta  \\\\\\\\  & ={∭}_{\\\\mathrm{Δ}}\\\\sqrt{{r}^{2}({\\\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\\\theta +{\\\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}\\\\theta )+1}\\\\cdot r\\\\hspace{0.222em}\\\\mathrm{d}z\\\\hspace{0.167em}\\\\mathrm{d}r\\\\hspace{0.167em}\\\\mathrm{d}\\\\theta  \\\\\\\\  & ={\\\\int }_{0}^{\\\\bbox[Gainsboro, 2pt]{{\\\\color{blue}\\\\pi /2}}}{\\\\int }_{0}^{8\\\\operatorname{sin}\\\\theta }{\\\\int }_{-5}^{\\\\bbox[Gainsboro, 2pt]{{\\\\color{blue}r\\\\operatorname{cos}\\\\theta }}}\\\\bbox[Gainsboro, 2pt]{{\\\\color{blue}r\\\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+1}}}\\\\hspace{0.222em}\\\\mathrm{d}z\\\\hspace{0.222em}\\\\text{d}r\\\\hspace{0.222em}\\\\text{d}\\\\theta .\\\\end{aligned}\\n$$\"} {:db/id 17592186536983 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid \"237e8e7d-e3db-5f7c-9347-4abfc31811d3\" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value \"{\\\\int }_{0}^{\\\\frac{\\\\pi}{2}}{\\\\int }_{0}^{4 \\\\cos{\\\\left(\\\\theta \\\\right)}}{\\\\int }_{0}^{1}r^{3}\\\\,dz\\\\,\\\\text{d}r\\\\,\\\\text{d}\\\\theta \" :db/id 17592186536996} {:answer/id #uuid \"4f83f8a6-2631-55b9-a475-cf21a17cb881\" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value \"{\\\\int }_{0}^{\\\\frac{\\\\pi}{2}}{\\\\int }_{0}^{4 \\\\cos{\\\\left(\\\\theta \\\\right)}}{\\\\int }_{1}^{0}r^{3}\\\\,dz\\\\,\\\\text{d}r\\\\,\\\\text{d}\\\\theta \" :db/id 17592186537015} {:answer/id #uuid \"9d691199-900d-5dbc-95ff-fd5da1703bb8\" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value \"{\\\\int }_{0}^{\\\\frac{\\\\pi}{2}}{\\\\int }_{0}^{4 \\\\cos{\\\\left(\\\\theta \\\\right)}}{\\\\int }_{0}^{1}r^{4}\\\\,dz\\\\,\\\\text{d}r\\\\,\\\\text{d}\\\\theta \" :db/id 17592186537043} {:answer/id #uuid \"b727e49c-dd3f-5d45-b030-cf65baa91799\" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value \"{\\\\int }_{0}^{\\\\frac{\\\\pi}{2}}{\\\\int }_{0}^{4 \\\\cos{\\\\left(\\\\theta \\\\right)}}{\\\\int }_{0}^{1}r^{2}\\\\,dz\\\\,\\\\text{d}r\\\\,\\\\text{d}\\\\theta \" :db/id 17592186537053} {:answer/id #uuid \"f8d59057-b6e1-58d4-8bca-71b7a0d1be8f\" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value \"{\\\\int }_{0}^{\\\\frac{\\\\pi}{2}}{\\\\int }_{0}^{2 \\\\cos{\\\\left(\\\\theta \\\\right)}}{\\\\int }_{0}^{1}r^{3}\\\\,dz\\\\,\\\\text{d}r\\\\,\\\\text{d}\\\\theta \" :db/id 17592186537076}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid \"237e8e7d-e3db-5f7c-9347-4abfc31811d3\" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value \"{\\\\int }_{0}^{\\\\frac{\\\\pi}{2}}{\\\\int }_{0}^{4 \\\\cos{\\\\left(\\\\theta \\\\right)}}{\\\\int }_{0}^{1}r^{3}\\\\,dz\\\\,\\\\text{d}r\\\\,\\\\text{d}\\\\theta \" :db/id 17592186536996} :answer-field/id #uuid \"fb63a029-3502-5e90-a9fa-8ddfb79c7c2d\" :answer-field/key \"selection\" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186537078}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid \"00623118-ee32-53b0-8e64-30db43b56fa6\" :question/problem \"If the solid region $R$ is given by \\n\\n$$\\nR=\\\\{(x,y,z)\\\\,:\\\\,(x-2)^{2}+{y}^{2}\\\\le 4,y\\\\ge 0,\\\\,0\\\\le z\\\\le 1\\\\},\\n$$\\n\\nthen express the triple integral\\n\\n$$\\n{∭}_{R}x^{2} + y^{2}\\\\,\\\\text{d}V\\n$$\\n\\nas an equivalent integral in cylindrical polar coordinates.\" :question/worked-solution \"We will use the change of variables formula in the form\\n\\n$$\\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\\\,dV={∭}_{Δ}\\\\,f(r\\\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\\\theta ,r\\\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\\\theta ,z)\\\\,r\\\\,dz\\\\,dr\\\\,d\\\\theta .\\n$$\\n\\nIf $(r,\\\\theta ,z)$ are the cylindrical polar coordinates of a point, then its Cartesian coordinates $(x,y,z)$ can be found by using the formulas\\n\\n$$\\nx=r\\\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\\\theta ,\\\\,y=r\\\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\\\theta ,\\\\,z=z.\\n$$\\n\\nFirst, let's sketch the projection of $R$ onto the $xy$-plane:\\n\\n![](images/d2/d2b3344b7b910f5685d4fd81b75a600608af6b008778ff608cb084edb5a92da9.png)\\n\\nThe region $R$ is one-half of a right vertical cylinder of radius $2$ whose axis passes through the point $(2,0)$ in the $xy$-plane, and is\\n\\n- bounded below by $z=0,$ and\\n\\n- bounded above by $z=1.$\\n\\nWith that in mind, let's now write the $(x-2)^{2}+{y}^{2}=4$ in polar coordinates:\\n\\n$$\\n\\\\begin{aligned}(x-2)^{2}+{y}^{2} & =4 \\\\\\\\ (r\\\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\\\theta-2)^{2}+(r\\\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\\\theta)^{2} & =4 \\\\\\\\ {r}^{2}{cos}^{2}⁡\\\\theta -4r\\\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\\\theta+4+{r}^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡\\\\theta & =4 \\\\\\\\ {r}^{2}({cos}^{2}⁡\\\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\\\theta )-4r\\\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\\\theta & =0 \\\\\\\\ {r}^{2}-4r\\\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\\\theta & =0 \\\\\\\\ r(r-4\\\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\\\theta) & =0\\\\end{aligned}\\n$$\\n\\nSince $r$ cannot be identical to zero on the entire semicircle, we must have $r=4 \\\\cos{\\\\left(\\\\theta \\\\right)}.$\\n\\nAs a result, in cylindrical polar coordinates, our region can be expressed as \\n\\n$$\\nΔ=\\\\{(r,\\\\theta ,z)\\\\,:\\\\,0\\\\le r\\\\le 4 \\\\cos{\\\\left(\\\\theta \\\\right)},\\\\,0\\\\le \\\\theta \\\\le \\\\frac{\\\\pi}{2},\\\\,0\\\\le z\\\\le 1\\\\}.\\n$$\\n\\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain\\n\\n$$\\n\\\\begin{aligned}{∭}_{R}x^{2} + y^{2}\\\\,\\\\text{d}V & ={∭}_{Δ}((r\\\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\\\theta)^{2}+(r\\\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\\\theta)^{2})\\\\cdot r\\\\,dz\\\\,dr\\\\,d\\\\theta \\\\\\\\  & ={∭}_{Δ}{r}^{2}({cos}^{2}⁡\\\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\\\theta )\\\\cdot r\\\\,dz\\\\,dr\\\\,d\\\\theta \\\\\\\\  & ={\\\\int }_{0}^{\\\\frac{\\\\pi}{2}}{\\\\int }_{0}^{4 \\\\cos{\\\\left(\\\\theta \\\\right)}}{\\\\int }_{0}^{1}r^{3}\\\\,dz\\\\,\\\\text{d}r\\\\,\\\\text{d}\\\\theta .\\\\end{aligned}\\n$$\"} {:db/id 17592186537003 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid \"30aa1de6-9825-58df-a0bb-9361d44a7568\" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value \"{\\\\int }_{\\\\pi}^{2 \\\\pi}{\\\\int }_{0}^{- 4 \\\\sin{\\\\left(\\\\theta \\\\right)}}{\\\\int }_{0}^{r^{2} + 5}r^{3} \\\\cos^{2}{\\\\left(\\\\theta \\\\right)}\\\\,dz\\\\,\\\\text{d}r\\\\,\\\\text{d}\\\\theta \" :db/id 17592186536998} {:answer/id #uuid \"32b99b29-cd14-5ea5-9fc1-15df5b4eb850\" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value \"{\\\\int }_{\\\\pi}^{2 \\\\pi}{\\\\int }_{0}^{- 4 \\\\sin{\\\\left(\\\\theta \\\\right)}}{\\\\int }_{0}^{r^{2} + 5}r^{3} \\\\sin{\\\\left(\\\\theta \\\\right)} \\\\cos{\\\\left(\\\\theta \\\\right)}\\\\,dz\\\\,\\\\text{d}r\\\\,\\\\text{d}\\\\theta \" :db/id 17592186537001} {:answer/id #uuid \"9605e9f5-cd80-5de4-9598-0eef1d67fdca\" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value \"{\\\\int }_{\\\\pi}^{2 \\\\pi}{\\\\int }_{0}^{- 4 \\\\sin{\\\\left(\\\\theta \\\\right)}}{\\\\int }_{0}^{r^{2} + 5}r^{2} \\\\sin^{2}{\\\\left(\\\\theta \\\\right)}\\\\,dz\\\\,\\\\text{d}r\\\\,\\\\text{d}\\\\theta \" :db/id 17592186537039} {:answer/id #uuid \"9c51e829-74a8-59f0-b024-a19f558413ab\" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value \"{\\\\int }_{\\\\pi}^{2 \\\\pi}{\\\\int }_{0}^{- 4 \\\\sin{\\\\left(\\\\theta \\\\right)}}{\\\\int }_{0}^{r^{2} + 5}r^{2} \\\\cos^{2}{\\\\left(\\\\theta \\\\right)}\\\\,dz\\\\,\\\\text{d}r\\\\,\\\\text{d}\\\\theta \" :db/id 17592186537042} {:answer/id #uuid \"dff20a83-a22c-52be-b879-f991bfe4e845\" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value \"{\\\\int }_{\\\\pi}^{2 \\\\pi}{\\\\int }_{0}^{- 4 \\\\sin{\\\\left(\\\\theta \\\\right)}}{\\\\int }_{0}^{r^{2} + 5}r^{3} \\\\sin^{2}{\\\\left(\\\\theta \\\\right)}\\\\,dz\\\\,\\\\text{d}r\\\\,\\\\text{d}\\\\theta \" :db/id 17592186537068}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid \"32b99b29-cd14-5ea5-9fc1-15df5b4eb850\" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value \"{\\\\int }_{\\\\pi}^{2 \\\\pi}{\\\\int }_{0}^{- 4 \\\\sin{\\\\left(\\\\theta \\\\right)}}{\\\\int }_{0}^{r^{2} + 5}r^{3} \\\\sin{\\\\left(\\\\theta \\\\right)} \\\\cos{\\\\left(\\\\theta \\\\right)}\\\\,dz\\\\,\\\\text{d}r\\\\,\\\\text{d}\\\\theta \" :db/id 17592186537001} :answer-field/id #uuid \"c677a795-89fc-56d8-8a2d-f295aa205e01\" :answer-field/key \"selection\" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186537056}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid \"3421c850-1e07-524f-a0e9-45c83fcec039\" :question/problem \"If the solid region $R$ is given by \\n\\n$$\\nR=\\\\{(x,y,z)\\\\,:\\\\,{x}^{2}+(y+2)^{2}\\\\le 4,\\\\,0\\\\le z\\\\le x^{2} + y^{2} + 5\\\\},\\n$$\\n\\nthen express the triple integral\\n\\n$$\\n{∭}_{R}x y\\\\,\\\\text{d}V\\n$$\\n\\nas an equivalent integral in cylindrical polar coordinates.\" :question/worked-solution \"We will use the change of variables formula in the form\\n\\n$$\\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\\\,dV={∭}_{Δ}\\\\,f(r\\\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\\\theta ,r\\\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\\\theta ,z)\\\\,r\\\\,dz\\\\,dr\\\\,d\\\\theta .\\n$$\\n\\nIf $(r,\\\\theta ,z)$ are the cylindrical polar coordinates of a point, then its Cartesian coordinates $(x,y,z)$ can be found by using the formulas\\n\\n$$\\nx=r\\\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\\\theta ,\\\\,y=r\\\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\\\theta ,\\\\,z=z.\\n$$\\n\\nFirst, let's sketch the projection of $R$ onto the $xy$-plane:\\n\\n![](images/ce/cee08173667c9577f4c7aab8cac5386434f2e1ecab8836150ebe8066d1cbaa68.png)\\n\\nThe region $R$ is a right vertical cylinder of radius $2$ whose axis passes through the point $(0,-2)$ in the $xy$-plane, and is\\n\\n- bounded below by $z=0,$ and\\n\\n- bounded above by $z=x^{2} + y^{2} + 5=r^{2} + 5.$\\n\\nWith that in mind, let's write the equation ${x}^{2}+(y+2)^{2}=4$ in polar coordinates:\\n\\n$$\\n\\\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+(y+2)^{2} & =4 \\\\\\\\ (r\\\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\\\theta)^{2}+(r\\\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\\\theta+2)^{2} & =4 \\\\\\\\ {r}^{2}{cos}^{2}⁡\\\\theta +{r}^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡\\\\theta +4r\\\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\\\theta+4 & =4 \\\\\\\\ {r}^{2}({cos}^{2}⁡\\\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\\\theta )+4r\\\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\\\theta & =0 \\\\\\\\ {r}^{2}+4r\\\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\\\theta & =0 \\\\\\\\ r(r+4\\\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\\\theta) & =0\\\\end{aligned}\\n$$\\n\\nSince $r$ cannot be identical to zero on the entire circle, we must have $r=- 4 \\\\sin{\\\\left(\\\\theta \\\\right)}.$\\n\\nAs a result, in cylindrical polar coordinates, our region can be expressed as \\n\\n$$\\nΔ=\\\\{(r,\\\\theta ,z)\\\\,:\\\\,0\\\\le r\\\\le - 4 \\\\sin{\\\\left(\\\\theta \\\\right)},\\\\,\\\\pi\\\\le \\\\theta \\\\le 2 \\\\pi,\\\\,0\\\\le z\\\\le r^{2} + 5\\\\}.\\n$$\\n\\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain\\n\\n$$\\n\\\\begin{aligned}{∭}_{R}x y\\\\,\\\\text{d}V & ={∭}_{Δ}(r\\\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\\\theta)(r\\\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\\\theta)\\\\cdot r\\\\,dz\\\\,dr\\\\,d\\\\theta \\\\\\\\  & ={\\\\int }_{\\\\pi}^{2 \\\\pi}{\\\\int }_{0}^{- 4 \\\\sin{\\\\left(\\\\theta \\\\right)}}{\\\\int }_{0}^{r^{2} + 5}r^{3} \\\\sin{\\\\left(\\\\theta \\\\right)} \\\\cos{\\\\left(\\\\theta \\\\right)}\\\\,dz\\\\,\\\\text{d}r\\\\,\\\\text{d}\\\\theta .\\\\end{aligned}\\n$$\"} {:db/id 17592186537006 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid \"321df476-4949-5c09-820e-9305d838bc49\" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value \"{\\\\int }_{\\\\frac{\\\\pi}{2}}^{\\\\pi}{\\\\int }_{0}^{8 \\\\sin{\\\\left(\\\\theta \\\\right)}}{\\\\int }_{-5}^{\\\\sqrt{81 - r^{2}}}2 r^{3} \\\\sin{\\\\left(\\\\theta \\\\right)} \\\\cos{\\\\left(\\\\theta \\\\right)}\\\\,dz\\\\,\\\\text{d}r\\\\,\\\\text{d}\\\\theta \" :db/id 17592186537000} {:answer/id #uuid \"4e59d9d4-da68-5e58-aff1-c4c4a3337e4c\" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value \"{\\\\int }_{\\\\frac{\\\\pi}{2}}^{\\\\pi}{\\\\int }_{0}^{8 \\\\sin{\\\\left(\\\\theta \\\\right)}}{\\\\int }_{-5}^{\\\\sqrt{81 - r^{2}}}2 r^{4} \\\\sin{\\\\left(\\\\theta \\\\right)} \\\\cos{\\\\left(\\\\theta \\\\right)}\\\\,dz\\\\,\\\\text{d}r\\\\,\\\\text{d}\\\\theta \" :db/id 17592186537014} {:answer/id #uuid \"9a68c5dc-993d-52d2-9754-5b09d9106e51\" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value \"{\\\\int }_{\\\\frac{\\\\pi}{2}}^{\\\\pi}{\\\\int }_{0}^{4 \\\\sin{\\\\left(\\\\theta \\\\right)}}{\\\\int }_{-5}^{\\\\sqrt{81 - r^{2}}}2 r^{3} \\\\sin{\\\\left(\\\\theta \\\\right)} \\\\cos{\\\\left(\\\\theta \\\\right)}\\\\,dz\\\\,\\\\text{d}r\\\\,\\\\text{d}\\\\theta \" :db/id 17592186537041} {:answer/id #uuid \"b4961f55-574a-53f6-91ac-244a2907cf1a\" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value \"{\\\\int }_{\\\\frac{\\\\pi}{2}}^{\\\\pi}{\\\\int }_{0}^{8 \\\\sin{\\\\left(\\\\theta \\\\right)}}{\\\\int }_{\\\\sqrt{81 - r^{2}}}^{-5}2 r^{3} \\\\sin{\\\\left(\\\\theta \\\\right)} \\\\cos{\\\\left(\\\\theta \\\\right)}\\\\,dz\\\\,\\\\text{d}r\\\\,\\\\text{d}\\\\theta \" :db/id 17592186537051} {:answer/id #uuid \"c82cc82c-e96d-5c71-9bfd-49718a58969e\" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value \"{\\\\int }_{\\\\frac{\\\\pi}{2}}^{\\\\pi}{\\\\int }_{0}^{8 \\\\sin{\\\\left(\\\\theta \\\\right)}}{\\\\int }_{-5}^{\\\\sqrt{81 - r^{2}}}2 r^{2} \\\\sin{\\\\left(\\\\theta \\\\right)} \\\\cos{\\\\left(\\\\theta \\\\right)}\\\\,dz\\\\,\\\\text{d}r\\\\,\\\\text{d}\\\\theta \" :db/id 17592186537059}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid \"321df476-4949-5c09-820e-9305d838bc49\" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value \"{\\\\int }_{\\\\frac{\\\\pi}{2}}^{\\\\pi}{\\\\int }_{0}^{8 \\\\sin{\\\\left(\\\\theta \\\\right)}}{\\\\int }_{-5}^{\\\\sqrt{81 - r^{2}}}2 r^{3} \\\\sin{\\\\left(\\\\theta \\\\right)} \\\\cos{\\\\left(\\\\theta \\\\right)}\\\\,dz\\\\,\\\\text{d}r\\\\,\\\\text{d}\\\\theta \" :db/id 17592186537000} :answer-field/id #uuid \"346ca32c-f65c-5df0-9d38-5355dcbfffd3\" :answer-field/key \"selection\" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186537004}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid \"3595f3ec-49d1-5481-82e6-c23464cfa603\" :question/problem \"If the solid region $R$ is given by \\n\\n$$\\nR=\\\\{(x,y,z)\\\\,:\\\\,{x}^{2}+(y-4)^{2}\\\\le 16,x\\\\le 0,\\\\,-5\\\\le z\\\\le \\\\sqrt{- x^{2} - y^{2} + 81}\\\\},\\n$$\\n\\nthen express the triple integral\\n\\n$$\\n{∭}_{R}2 x y\\\\,\\\\text{d}V\\n$$\\n\\nas an equivalent integral in cylindrical polar coordinates.\" :question/worked-solution \"We will use the change of variables formula in the form\\n\\n$$\\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\\\,dV={∭}_{Δ}\\\\,f(r\\\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\\\theta ,r\\\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\\\theta ,z)\\\\,r\\\\,dz\\\\,dr\\\\,d\\\\theta .\\n$$\\n\\nIf $(r,\\\\theta ,z)$ are the cylindrical polar coordinates of a point, then its Cartesian coordinates $(x,y,z)$ can be found by using the formulas\\n\\n$$\\nx=r\\\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\\\theta ,\\\\,y=r\\\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\\\theta ,\\\\,z=z.\\n$$\\n\\nFirst, let's sketch the projection of $R$ onto the $xy$-plane:\\n\\n![](images/57/5764f328a614ddaa96abd28a4568c4cd7dfcf0988033d3122bdce0e1e52b7bc5.png)\\n\\nThe region $R$ is one-half of a right vertical cylinder of radius $4$ whose axis passes through the point $(0,4)$ in the $xy$-plane, and is\\n\\n- bounded below by $z=-5,$ and\\n\\n- bounded above by $z=\\\\sqrt{- x^{2} - y^{2} + 81}=\\\\sqrt{81 - r^{2}}.$\\n\\nWith that in mind, let's now write the ${x}^{2}+(y-4)^{2}=16$ in polar coordinates:\\n\\n$$\\n\\\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+(y-4)^{2} & =16 \\\\\\\\ (r\\\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\\\theta)^{2}+(r\\\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\\\theta-4)^{2} & =16 \\\\\\\\ {r}^{2}{cos}^{2}⁡\\\\theta +{r}^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡\\\\theta -8r\\\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\\\theta+16 & =16 \\\\\\\\ {r}^{2}({cos}^{2}⁡\\\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\\\theta )-8r\\\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\\\theta & =0 \\\\\\\\ {r}^{2}-8r\\\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\\\theta & =0 \\\\\\\\ r(r-8\\\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\\\theta) & =0\\\\end{aligned}\\n$$\\n\\nSince $r$ cannot be identical to zero on the entire semicircle, we must have $r=8 \\\\sin{\\\\left(\\\\theta \\\\right)}.$\\n\\nAs a result, in cylindrical polar coordinates, our region can be expressed as \\n\\n$$\\nΔ=\\\\{(r,\\\\theta ,z)\\\\,:\\\\,0\\\\le r\\\\le 8 \\\\sin{\\\\left(\\\\theta \\\\right)},\\\\,\\\\frac{\\\\pi}{2}\\\\le \\\\theta \\\\le \\\\pi,\\\\,-5\\\\le z\\\\le \\\\sqrt{81 - r^{2}}\\\\}.\\n$$\\n\\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain\\n\\n$$\\n\\\\begin{aligned}{∭}_{R}2 x y\\\\,\\\\text{d}V & ={∭}_{Δ}2(r\\\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\\\theta)(r\\\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\\\theta)\\\\cdot r\\\\,dz\\\\,dr\\\\,d\\\\theta \\\\\\\\  & ={\\\\int }_{\\\\frac{\\\\pi}{2}}^{\\\\pi}{\\\\int }_{0}^{8 \\\\sin{\\\\left(\\\\theta \\\\right)}}{\\\\int }_{-5}^{\\\\sqrt{81 - r^{2}}}2 r^{3} \\\\sin{\\\\left(\\\\theta \\\\right)} \\\\cos{\\\\left(\\\\theta \\\\right)}\\\\,dz\\\\,\\\\text{d}r\\\\,\\\\text{d}\\\\theta .\\\\end{aligned}\\n$$\"} {:db/id 17592186537019 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid \"30efa546-da45-5a01-977f-f55cd5e7ba61\" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value \"{\\\\int }_{0}^{\\\\frac{\\\\pi}{2}}{\\\\int }_{0}^{4 \\\\cos{\\\\left(\\\\theta \\\\right)}}{\\\\int }_{4}^{7}r^{3}\\\\,dz\\\\,\\\\text{d}r\\\\,\\\\text{d}\\\\theta \" :db/id 17592186536999} {:answer/id #uuid \"3dc4f541-1cf6-594c-ba21-8359f5bbbc24\" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value \"{\\\\int }_{0}^{\\\\frac{\\\\pi}{2}}{\\\\int }_{0}^{4 \\\\cos{\\\\left(\\\\theta \\\\right)}}{\\\\int }_{7}^{4}r^{3}\\\\,dz\\\\,\\\\text{d}r\\\\,\\\\text{d}\\\\theta \" :db/id 17592186537011} {:answer/id #uuid \"7a324aba-9b63-5874-a2b1-733a50c7781b\" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value \"{\\\\int }_{0}^{\\\\frac{\\\\pi}{2}}{\\\\int }_{0}^{4 \\\\cos{\\\\left(\\\\theta \\\\right)}}{\\\\int }_{4}^{7}r^{4}\\\\,dz\\\\,\\\\text{d}r\\\\,\\\\text{d}\\\\theta \" :db/id 17592186537033} {:answer/id #uuid \"81de05b4-209e-5bc0-9833-10807223dd7a\" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value \"{\\\\int }_{0}^{\\\\frac{\\\\pi}{2}}{\\\\int }_{0}^{2 \\\\cos{\\\\left(\\\\theta \\\\right)}}{\\\\int }_{4}^{7}r^{3}\\\\,dz\\\\,\\\\text{d}r\\\\,\\\\text{d}\\\\theta \" :db/id 17592186537035} {:answer/id #uuid \"e388a7a5-3cdc-52bc-8ddf-defa6c22aae8\" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value \"{\\\\int }_{0}^{\\\\frac{\\\\pi}{2}}{\\\\int }_{0}^{4 \\\\cos{\\\\left(\\\\theta \\\\right)}}{\\\\int }_{4}^{7}r^{2}\\\\,dz\\\\,\\\\text{d}r\\\\,\\\\text{d}\\\\theta \" :db/id 17592186537070}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid \"30efa546-da45-5a01-977f-f55cd5e7ba61\" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value \"{\\\\int }_{0}^{\\\\frac{\\\\pi}{2}}{\\\\int }_{0}^{4 \\\\cos{\\\\left(\\\\theta \\\\right)}}{\\\\int }_{4}^{7}r^{3}\\\\,dz\\\\,\\\\text{d}r\\\\,\\\\text{d}\\\\theta \" :db/id 17592186536999} :answer-field/id #uuid \"65f21aac-e7ac-5168-a171-1b5b4f594e1c\" :answer-field/key \"selection\" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186537022}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid \"590500ab-ca5a-598f-be1f-2910c46fae9a\" :question/problem \"If the solid region $R$ is given by \\n\\n$$\\nR=\\\\{(x,y,z)\\\\,:\\\\,(x-2)^{2}+{y}^{2}\\\\le 4,y\\\\ge 0,\\\\,4\\\\le z\\\\le 7\\\\},\\n$$\\n\\nthen express the triple integral\\n\\n$$\\n{∭}_{R}x^{2} + y^{2}\\\\,\\\\text{d}V\\n$$\\n\\nas an equivalent integral in cylindrical polar coordinates.\" :question/worked-solution \"We will use the change of variables formula in the form\\n\\n$$\\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\\\,dV={∭}_{Δ}\\\\,f(r\\\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\\\theta ,r\\\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\\\theta ,z)\\\\,r\\\\,dz\\\\,dr\\\\,d\\\\theta .\\n$$\\n\\nIf $(r,\\\\theta ,z)$ are the cylindrical polar coordinates of a point, then its Cartesian coordinates $(x,y,z)$ can be found by using the formulas\\n\\n$$\\nx=r\\\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\\\theta ,\\\\,y=r\\\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\\\theta ,\\\\,z=z.\\n$$\\n\\nFirst, let's sketch the projection of $R$ onto the $xy$-plane:\\n\\n![](images/d2/d2b3344b7b910f5685d4fd81b75a600608af6b008778ff608cb084edb5a92da9.png)\\n\\nThe region $R$ is one-half of a right vertical cylinder of radius $2$ whose axis passes through the point $(2,0)$ in the $xy$-plane, and is\\n\\n- bounded below by $z=4,$ and\\n\\n- bounded above by $z=7.$\\n\\nWith that in mind, let's now write the $(x-2)^{2}+{y}^{2}=4$ in polar coordinates:\\n\\n$$\\n\\\\begin{aligned}(x-2)^{2}+{y}^{2} & =4 \\\\\\\\ (r\\\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\\\theta-2)^{2}+(r\\\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\\\theta)^{2} & =4 \\\\\\\\ {r}^{2}{cos}^{2}⁡\\\\theta -4r\\\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\\\theta+4+{r}^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡\\\\theta & =4 \\\\\\\\ {r}^{2}({cos}^{2}⁡\\\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\\\theta )-4r\\\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\\\theta & =0 \\\\\\\\ {r}^{2}-4r\\\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\\\theta & =0 \\\\\\\\ r(r-4\\\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\\\theta) & =0\\\\end{aligned}\\n$$\\n\\nSince $r$ cannot be identical to zero on the entire semicircle, we must have $r=4 \\\\cos{\\\\left(\\\\theta \\\\right)}.$\\n\\nAs a result, in cylindrical polar coordinates, our region can be expressed as \\n\\n$$\\nΔ=\\\\{(r,\\\\theta ,z)\\\\,:\\\\,0\\\\le r\\\\le 4 \\\\cos{\\\\left(\\\\theta \\\\right)},\\\\,0\\\\le \\\\theta \\\\le \\\\frac{\\\\pi}{2},\\\\,4\\\\le z\\\\le 7\\\\}.\\n$$\\n\\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain\\n\\n$$\\n\\\\begin{aligned}{∭}_{R}x^{2} + y^{2}\\\\,\\\\text{d}V & ={∭}_{Δ}((r\\\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\\\theta)^{2}+(r\\\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\\\theta)^{2})\\\\cdot r\\\\,dz\\\\,dr\\\\,d\\\\theta \\\\\\\\  & ={∭}_{Δ}{r}^{2}({cos}^{2}⁡\\\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\\\theta )\\\\cdot r\\\\,dz\\\\,dr\\\\,d\\\\theta \\\\\\\\  & ={\\\\int }_{0}^{\\\\frac{\\\\pi}{2}}{\\\\int }_{0}^{4 \\\\cos{\\\\left(\\\\theta \\\\right)}}{\\\\int }_{4}^{7}r^{3}\\\\,dz\\\\,\\\\text{d}r\\\\,\\\\text{d}\\\\theta .\\\\end{aligned}\\n$$\"} {:db/id 17592186537025 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid \"0da30d8b-b4d8-596a-8c80-6b14e0374096\" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value \"{\\\\int }_{0}^{\\\\frac{\\\\pi}{2}}{\\\\int }_{0}^{8 \\\\cos{\\\\left(\\\\theta \\\\right)}}{\\\\int }_{4}^{7}r^{2}\\\\,dz\\\\,\\\\text{d}r\\\\,\\\\text{d}\\\\theta \" :db/id 17592186536987} {:answer/id #uuid \"0e208e2f-cf97-52a6-913e-893119de1c31\" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value \"{\\\\int }_{0}^{\\\\frac{\\\\pi}{2}}{\\\\int }_{0}^{8 \\\\cos{\\\\left(\\\\theta \\\\right)}}{\\\\int }_{4}^{7}r^{3}\\\\,dz\\\\,\\\\text{d}r\\\\,\\\\text{d}\\\\theta \" :db/id 17592186536988} {:answer/id #uuid \"20ec8899-9f98-5939-b1be-717c8a36b84e\" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value \"{\\\\int }_{0}^{\\\\frac{\\\\pi}{2}}{\\\\int }_{0}^{8 \\\\cos{\\\\left(\\\\theta \\\\right)}}{\\\\int }_{4}^{7}r^{4}\\\\,dz\\\\,\\\\text{d}r\\\\,\\\\text{d}\\\\theta \" :db/id 17592186536994} {:answer/id #uuid \"37eefddf-45ba-5950-8013-940c638fd35a\" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value \"{\\\\int }_{0}^{\\\\frac{\\\\pi}{2}}{\\\\int }_{0}^{8 \\\\cos{\\\\left(\\\\theta \\\\right)}}{\\\\int }_{7}^{4}r^{3}\\\\,dz\\\\,\\\\text{d}r\\\\,\\\\text{d}\\\\theta \" :db/id 17592186537007} {:answer/id #uuid \"9f831628-be52-5b60-9299-337a30d46fe6\" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value \"{\\\\int }_{0}^{\\\\frac{\\\\pi}{2}}{\\\\int }_{0}^{4 \\\\cos{\\\\left(\\\\theta \\\\right)}}{\\\\int }_{4}^{7}r^{3}\\\\,dz\\\\,\\\\text{d}r\\\\,\\\\text{d}\\\\theta \" :db/id 17592186537044}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid \"0e208e2f-cf97-52a6-913e-893119de1c31\" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value \"{\\\\int }_{0}^{\\\\frac{\\\\pi}{2}}{\\\\int }_{0}^{8 \\\\cos{\\\\left(\\\\theta \\\\right)}}{\\\\int }_{4}^{7}r^{3}\\\\,dz\\\\,\\\\text{d}r\\\\,\\\\text{d}\\\\theta \" :db/id 17592186536988} :answer-field/id #uuid \"3b7df119-1e10-5a12-b44e-c332dcfb8b0a\" :answer-field/key \"selection\" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186537008}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid \"69ed0333-f7ae-5aef-adf8-ef1e138c3825\" :question/problem \"If the solid region $R$ is given by \\n\\n$$\\nR=\\\\{(x,y,z)\\\\,:\\\\,(x-4)^{2}+{y}^{2}\\\\le 16,y\\\\ge 0,\\\\,4\\\\le z\\\\le 7\\\\},\\n$$\\n\\nthen express the triple integral\\n\\n$$\\n{∭}_{R}x^{2} + y^{2}\\\\,\\\\text{d}V\\n$$\\n\\nas an equivalent integral in cylindrical polar coordinates.\" :question/worked-solution \"We will use the change of variables formula in the form\\n\\n$$\\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\\\,dV={∭}_{Δ}\\\\,f(r\\\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\\\theta ,r\\\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\\\theta ,z)\\\\,r\\\\,dz\\\\,dr\\\\,d\\\\theta .\\n$$\\n\\nIf $(r,\\\\theta ,z)$ are the cylindrical polar coordinates of a point, then its Cartesian coordinates $(x,y,z)$ can be found by using the formulas\\n\\n$$\\nx=r\\\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\\\theta ,\\\\,y=r\\\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\\\theta ,\\\\,z=z.\\n$$\\n\\nFirst, let's sketch the projection of $R$ onto the $xy$-plane:\\n\\n![](images/80/80a919b28ae7ed559b06b34bd8512859794a7d6550970b56347e2deb653bf76d.png)\\n\\nThe region $R$ is one-half of a right vertical cylinder of radius $4$ whose axis passes through the point $(4,0)$ in the $xy$-plane, and is\\n\\n- bounded below by $z=4,$ and\\n\\n- bounded above by $z=7.$\\n\\nWith that in mind, let's now write the $(x-4)^{2}+{y}^{2}=16$ in polar coordinates:\\n\\n$$\\n\\\\begin{aligned}(x-4)^{2}+{y}^{2} & =16 \\\\\\\\ (r\\\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\\\theta-4)^{2}+(r\\\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\\\theta)^{2} & =16 \\\\\\\\ {r}^{2}{cos}^{2}⁡\\\\theta -8r\\\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\\\theta+16+{r}^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡\\\\theta & =16 \\\\\\\\ {r}^{2}({cos}^{2}⁡\\\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\\\theta )-8r\\\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\\\theta & =0 \\\\\\\\ {r}^{2}-8r\\\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\\\theta & =0 \\\\\\\\ r(r-8\\\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\\\theta) & =0\\\\end{aligned}\\n$$\\n\\nSince $r$ cannot be identical to zero on the entire semicircle, we must have $r=8 \\\\cos{\\\\left(\\\\theta \\\\right)}.$\\n\\nAs a result, in cylindrical polar coordinates, our region can be expressed as \\n\\n$$\\nΔ=\\\\{(r,\\\\theta ,z)\\\\,:\\\\,0\\\\le r\\\\le 8 \\\\cos{\\\\left(\\\\theta \\\\right)},\\\\,0\\\\le \\\\theta \\\\le \\\\frac{\\\\pi}{2},\\\\,4\\\\le z\\\\le 7\\\\}.\\n$$\\n\\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain\\n\\n$$\\n\\\\begin{aligned}{∭}_{R}x^{2} + y^{2}\\\\,\\\\text{d}V & ={∭}_{Δ}((r\\\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\\\theta)^{2}+(r\\\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\\\theta)^{2})\\\\cdot r\\\\,dz\\\\,dr\\\\,d\\\\theta \\\\\\\\  & ={∭}_{Δ}{r}^{2}({cos}^{2}⁡\\\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\\\theta )\\\\cdot r\\\\,dz\\\\,dr\\\\,d\\\\theta \\\\\\\\  & ={\\\\int }_{0}^{\\\\frac{\\\\pi}{2}}{\\\\int }_{0}^{8 \\\\cos{\\\\left(\\\\theta \\\\right)}}{\\\\int }_{4}^{7}r^{3}\\\\,dz\\\\,\\\\text{d}r\\\\,\\\\text{d}\\\\theta .\\\\end{aligned}\\n$$\"} {:db/id 17592186537027 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid \"6e702de4-81fb-57f8-b700-52c7a2d5b037\" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value \"{\\\\int }_{\\\\frac{\\\\pi}{2}}^{\\\\frac{3 \\\\pi}{2}}{\\\\int }_{0}^{- 4 \\\\cos{\\\\left(\\\\theta \\\\right)}}{\\\\int }_{r \\\\cos{\\\\left(\\\\theta \\\\right)} + 5}^{-1}\\\\frac{r}{\\\\sqrt{r^{2} + 2}}\\\\,dz\\\\,\\\\text{d}r\\\\,\\\\text{d}\\\\theta \" :db/id 17592186537029} {:answer/id #uuid \"6eef909b-2ae7-54ad-8347-4b8012df300a\" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value \"{\\\\int }_{\\\\frac{\\\\pi}{2}}^{\\\\frac{3 \\\\pi}{2}}{\\\\int }_{0}^{- 4 \\\\cos{\\\\left(\\\\theta \\\\right)}}{\\\\int }_{-1}^{r \\\\cos{\\\\left(\\\\theta \\\\right)} + 5}\\\\frac{r}{\\\\sqrt{r^{2} + 2}}\\\\,dz\\\\,\\\\text{d}r\\\\,\\\\text{d}\\\\theta \" :db/id 17592186537030} {:answer/id #uuid \"a4798cdd-ba94-5538-b8db-9acc2dd4aebf\" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value \"{\\\\int }_{\\\\frac{\\\\pi}{2}}^{\\\\frac{3 \\\\pi}{2}}{\\\\int }_{0}^{- 4 \\\\cos{\\\\left(\\\\theta \\\\right)}}{\\\\int }_{-1}^{r \\\\cos{\\\\left(\\\\theta \\\\right)} + 5}\\\\frac{1}{\\\\sqrt{r^{2} + 2}}\\\\,dz\\\\,\\\\text{d}r\\\\,\\\\text{d}\\\\theta \" :db/id 17592186537046} {:answer/id #uuid \"d2013466-8cd9-50cf-ab08-7466182070c0\" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value \"{\\\\int }_{\\\\frac{\\\\pi}{2}}^{\\\\frac{3 \\\\pi}{2}}{\\\\int }_{0}^{- 2 \\\\cos{\\\\left(\\\\theta \\\\right)}}{\\\\int }_{-1}^{r \\\\cos{\\\\left(\\\\theta \\\\right)} + 5}\\\\frac{r}{\\\\sqrt{r^{2} + 2}}\\\\,dz\\\\,\\\\text{d}r\\\\,\\\\text{d}\\\\theta \" :db/id 17592186537061} {:answer/id #uuid \"f189cf4d-34b4-53e8-8fda-d1dc11be310f\" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value \"{\\\\int }_{\\\\frac{\\\\pi}{2}}^{\\\\frac{3 \\\\pi}{2}}{\\\\int }_{0}^{- 4 \\\\cos{\\\\left(\\\\theta \\\\right)}}{\\\\int }_{-1}^{r \\\\cos{\\\\left(\\\\theta \\\\right)} + 5}\\\\frac{r^{2}}{\\\\sqrt{r^{2} + 2}}\\\\,dz\\\\,\\\\text{d}r\\\\,\\\\text{d}\\\\theta \" :db/id 17592186537073}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid \"6eef909b-2ae7-54ad-8347-4b8012df300a\" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value \"{\\\\int }_{\\\\frac{\\\\pi}{2}}^{\\\\frac{3 \\\\pi}{2}}{\\\\int }_{0}^{- 4 \\\\cos{\\\\left(\\\\theta \\\\right)}}{\\\\int }_{-1}^{r \\\\cos{\\\\left(\\\\theta \\\\right)} + 5}\\\\frac{r}{\\\\sqrt{r^{2} + 2}}\\\\,dz\\\\,\\\\text{d}r\\\\,\\\\text{d}\\\\theta \" :db/id 17592186537030} :answer-field/id #uuid \"1e63057c-fad2-57b0-ab28-4c0735601f0a\" :answer-field/key \"selection\" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186536991}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid \"6a4d1aae-ff57-5336-93e6-e2feb480e2e3\" :question/problem \"If the solid region $R$ is given by \\n\\n$$\\nR=\\\\{(x,y,z)\\\\,:\\\\,(x+2)^{2}+{y}^{2}\\\\le 4,\\\\,-1\\\\le z\\\\le x + 5\\\\},\\n$$\\n\\nthen express the triple integral\\n\\n$$\\n{∭}_{R}\\\\frac{1}{\\\\sqrt{x^{2} + y^{2} + 2}}\\\\,\\\\text{d}V\\n$$\\n\\nas an equivalent integral in cylindrical polar coordinates.\" :question/worked-solution \"We will use the change of variables formula in the form\\n\\n$$\\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\\\,dV={∭}_{Δ}\\\\,f(r\\\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\\\theta ,r\\\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\\\theta ,z)\\\\,r\\\\,dz\\\\,dr\\\\,d\\\\theta .\\n$$\\n\\nIf $(r,\\\\theta ,z)$ are the cylindrical polar coordinates of a point, then its Cartesian coordinates $(x,y,z)$ can be found by using the formulas\\n\\n$$\\nx=r\\\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\\\theta ,\\\\,y=r\\\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\\\theta ,\\\\,z=z.\\n$$\\n\\nFirst, let's sketch the projection of $R$ onto the $xy$-plane:\\n\\n![](images/f8/f830c1469527c615d213ffcd11e472083c77d7e5fbc8c1577a85244fb5739fc4.png)\\n\\nThe region $R$ is a right vertical cylinder of radius $2$ whose axis passes through the point $(-2,0)$ in the $xy$-plane, and is\\n\\n- bounded below by $z=-1,$ and\\n\\n- bounded above by $z=x + 5=r \\\\cos{\\\\left(\\\\theta \\\\right)} + 5.$\\n\\nWith that in mind, let's write the equation $(x+2)^{2}+{y}^{2}=4$ in polar coordinates:\\n\\n$$\\n\\\\begin{aligned}(x+2)^{2}+{y}^{2} & =4 \\\\\\\\ (r\\\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\\\theta+2)^{2}+(r\\\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\\\theta)^{2} & =4 \\\\\\\\ {r}^{2}{cos}^{2}⁡\\\\theta +4r\\\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\\\theta+4+{r}^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡\\\\theta & =4 \\\\\\\\ {r}^{2}({cos}^{2}⁡\\\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\\\theta )+4r\\\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\\\theta & =0 \\\\\\\\ {r}^{2}+4r\\\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\\\theta & =0 \\\\\\\\ r(r+4\\\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\\\theta) & =0\\\\end{aligned}\\n$$\\n\\nSince $r$ cannot be identical to zero on the entire circle, we must have $r=- 4 \\\\cos{\\\\left(\\\\theta \\\\right)}.$\\n\\nAs a result, in cylindrical polar coordinates, our region can be expressed as \\n\\n$$\\nΔ=\\\\{(r,\\\\theta ,z)\\\\,:\\\\,0\\\\le r\\\\le - 4 \\\\cos{\\\\left(\\\\theta \\\\right)},\\\\,\\\\frac{\\\\pi}{2}\\\\le \\\\theta \\\\le \\\\frac{3 \\\\pi}{2},\\\\,-1\\\\le z\\\\le r \\\\cos{\\\\left(\\\\theta \\\\right)} + 5\\\\}.\\n$$\\n\\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain  \\n\\n$$\\n\\\\begin{aligned}{∭}_{R}\\\\frac{1}{\\\\sqrt{x^{2} + y^{2} + 2}}\\\\,\\\\text{d}V & ={∭}_{Δ}\\\\frac{1}{\\\\sqrt{(r\\\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\\\theta)^{2}+(r\\\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\\\theta)^{2}+2}}\\\\cdot r\\\\,dz\\\\,dr\\\\,d\\\\theta \\\\\\\\  & ={∭}_{Δ}\\\\frac{r}{\\\\sqrt{{r}^{2}({cos}^{2}⁡\\\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\\\theta )+2}}\\\\,dz\\\\,dr\\\\,d\\\\theta \\\\\\\\  & ={\\\\int }_{\\\\frac{\\\\pi}{2}}^{\\\\frac{3 \\\\pi}{2}}{\\\\int }_{0}^{- 4 \\\\cos{\\\\left(\\\\theta \\\\right)}}{\\\\int }_{-1}^{r \\\\cos{\\\\left(\\\\theta \\\\right)} + 5}\\\\frac{r}{\\\\sqrt{r^{2} + 2}}\\\\,dz\\\\,\\\\text{d}r\\\\,\\\\text{d}\\\\theta .\\\\end{aligned}\\n$$\"} {:db/id 17592186537040 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid \"0525826c-a736-5227-9f0a-27dd1c50c845\" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value \"{\\\\int }_{\\\\frac{\\\\pi}{2}}^{\\\\frac{3 \\\\pi}{2}}{\\\\int }_{0}^{- 2 \\\\cos{\\\\left(\\\\theta \\\\right)}}{\\\\int }_{-4}^{r \\\\cos{\\\\left(\\\\theta \\\\right)} + 1}\\\\frac{r}{\\\\sqrt{r^{2} + 2}}\\\\,dz\\\\,\\\\text{d}r\\\\,\\\\text{d}\\\\theta \" :db/id 17592186536984} {:answer/id #uuid \"202eb17e-51e1-5247-9d96-d74c13ad2578\" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value \"{\\\\int }_{\\\\frac{\\\\pi}{2}}^{\\\\frac{3 \\\\pi}{2}}{\\\\int }_{0}^{- 4 \\\\cos{\\\\left(\\\\theta \\\\right)}}{\\\\int }_{-4}^{r \\\\cos{\\\\left(\\\\theta \\\\right)} + 1}\\\\frac{r^{2}}{\\\\sqrt{r^{2} + 2}}\\\\,dz\\\\,\\\\text{d}r\\\\,\\\\text{d}\\\\theta \" :db/id 17592186536992} {:answer/id #uuid \"ccfe631f-2545-5c41-af46-41c806290c74\" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value \"{\\\\int }_{\\\\frac{\\\\pi}{2}}^{\\\\frac{3 \\\\pi}{2}}{\\\\int }_{0}^{- 4 \\\\cos{\\\\left(\\\\theta \\\\right)}}{\\\\int }_{r \\\\cos{\\\\left(\\\\theta \\\\right)} + 1}^{-4}\\\\frac{r}{\\\\sqrt{r^{2} + 2}}\\\\,dz\\\\,\\\\text{d}r\\\\,\\\\text{d}\\\\theta \" :db/id 17592186537060} {:answer/id #uuid \"d3a68dfb-291a-522b-9d54-5f6067f6ec23\" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value \"{\\\\int }_{\\\\frac{\\\\pi}{2}}^{\\\\frac{3 \\\\pi}{2}}{\\\\int }_{0}^{- 4 \\\\cos{\\\\left(\\\\theta \\\\right)}}{\\\\int }_{-4}^{r \\\\cos{\\\\left(\\\\theta \\\\right)} + 1}\\\\frac{r}{\\\\sqrt{r^{2} + 2}}\\\\,dz\\\\,\\\\text{d}r\\\\,\\\\text{d}\\\\theta \" :db/id 17592186537063} {:answer/id #uuid \"f764cb4a-e42e-5109-87ae-915856bb8816\" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value \"{\\\\int }_{\\\\frac{\\\\pi}{2}}^{\\\\frac{3 \\\\pi}{2}}{\\\\int }_{0}^{- 4 \\\\cos{\\\\left(\\\\theta \\\\right)}}{\\\\int }_{-4}^{r \\\\cos{\\\\left(\\\\theta \\\\right)} + 1}\\\\frac{1}{\\\\sqrt{r^{2} + 2}}\\\\,dz\\\\,\\\\text{d}r\\\\,\\\\text{d}\\\\theta \" :db/id 17592186537075}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid \"d3a68dfb-291a-522b-9d54-5f6067f6ec23\" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value \"{\\\\int }_{\\\\frac{\\\\pi}{2}}^{\\\\frac{3 \\\\pi}{2}}{\\\\int }_{0}^{- 4 \\\\cos{\\\\left(\\\\theta \\\\right)}}{\\\\int }_{-4}^{r \\\\cos{\\\\left(\\\\theta \\\\right)} + 1}\\\\frac{r}{\\\\sqrt{r^{2} + 2}}\\\\,dz\\\\,\\\\text{d}r\\\\,\\\\text{d}\\\\theta \" :db/id 17592186537063} :answer-field/id #uuid \"fc962529-0553-5bdb-9bba-afa4db3dcd57\" :answer-field/key \"selection\" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186537080}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid \"9786603b-57ce-5b6a-ad81-096bf72b5c29\" :question/problem \"If the solid region $R$ is given by \\n\\n$$\\nR=\\\\{(x,y,z)\\\\,:\\\\,(x+2)^{2}+{y}^{2}\\\\le 4,\\\\,-4\\\\le z\\\\le x + 1\\\\},\\n$$\\n\\nthen express the triple integral\\n\\n$$\\n{∭}_{R}\\\\frac{1}{\\\\sqrt{x^{2} + y^{2} + 2}}\\\\,\\\\text{d}V\\n$$\\n\\nas an equivalent integral in cylindrical polar coordinates.\" :question/worked-solution \"We will use the change of variables formula in the form\\n\\n$$\\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\\\,dV={∭}_{Δ}\\\\,f(r\\\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\\\theta ,r\\\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\\\theta ,z)\\\\,r\\\\,dz\\\\,dr\\\\,d\\\\theta .\\n$$\\n\\nIf $(r,\\\\theta ,z)$ are the cylindrical polar coordinates of a point, then its Cartesian coordinates $(x,y,z)$ can be found by using the formulas\\n\\n$$\\nx=r\\\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\\\theta ,\\\\,y=r\\\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\\\theta ,\\\\,z=z.\\n$$\\n\\nFirst, let's sketch the projection of $R$ onto the $xy$-plane:\\n\\n![](images/f8/f830c1469527c615d213ffcd11e472083c77d7e5fbc8c1577a85244fb5739fc4.png)\\n\\nThe region $R$ is a right vertical cylinder of radius $2$ whose axis passes through the point $(-2,0)$ in the $xy$-plane, and is\\n\\n- bounded below by $z=-4,$ and\\n\\n- bounded above by $z=x + 1=r \\\\cos{\\\\left(\\\\theta \\\\right)} + 1.$\\n\\nWith that in mind, let's write the equation $(x+2)^{2}+{y}^{2}=4$ in polar coordinates:\\n\\n$$\\n\\\\begin{aligned}(x+2)^{2}+{y}^{2} & =4 \\\\\\\\ (r\\\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\\\theta+2)^{2}+(r\\\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\\\theta)^{2} & =4 \\\\\\\\ {r}^{2}{cos}^{2}⁡\\\\theta +4r\\\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\\\theta+4+{r}^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡\\\\theta & =4 \\\\\\\\ {r}^{2}({cos}^{2}⁡\\\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\\\theta )+4r\\\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\\\theta & =0 \\\\\\\\ {r}^{2}+4r\\\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\\\theta & =0 \\\\\\\\ r(r+4\\\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\\\theta) & =0\\\\end{aligned}\\n$$\\n\\nSince $r$ cannot be identical to zero on the entire circle, we must have $r=- 4 \\\\cos{\\\\left(\\\\theta \\\\right)}.$\\n\\nAs a result, in cylindrical polar coordinates, our region can be expressed as \\n\\n$$\\nΔ=\\\\{(r,\\\\theta ,z)\\\\,:\\\\,0\\\\le r\\\\le - 4 \\\\cos{\\\\left(\\\\theta \\\\right)},\\\\,\\\\frac{\\\\pi}{2}\\\\le \\\\theta \\\\le \\\\frac{3 \\\\pi}{2},\\\\,-4\\\\le z\\\\le r \\\\cos{\\\\left(\\\\theta \\\\right)} + 1\\\\}.\\n$$\\n\\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain  \\n\\n$$\\n\\\\begin{aligned}{∭}_{R}\\\\frac{1}{\\\\sqrt{x^{2} + y^{2} + 2}}\\\\,\\\\text{d}V & ={∭}_{Δ}\\\\frac{1}{\\\\sqrt{(r\\\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\\\theta)^{2}+(r\\\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\\\theta)^{2}+2}}\\\\cdot r\\\\,dz\\\\,dr\\\\,d\\\\theta \\\\\\\\  & ={∭}_{Δ}\\\\frac{r}{\\\\sqrt{{r}^{2}({cos}^{2}⁡\\\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\\\theta )+2}}\\\\,dz\\\\,dr\\\\,d\\\\theta \\\\\\\\  & ={\\\\int }_{\\\\frac{\\\\pi}{2}}^{\\\\frac{3 \\\\pi}{2}}{\\\\int }_{0}^{- 4 \\\\cos{\\\\left(\\\\theta \\\\right)}}{\\\\int }_{-4}^{r \\\\cos{\\\\left(\\\\theta \\\\right)} + 1}\\\\frac{r}{\\\\sqrt{r^{2} + 2}}\\\\,dz\\\\,\\\\text{d}r\\\\,\\\\text{d}\\\\theta .\\\\end{aligned}\\n$$\"} {:db/id 17592186537047 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid \"4b0e90e7-4add-5e33-8547-4c25dae10c4b\" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value \"{\\\\int }_{\\\\frac{\\\\pi}{2}}^{\\\\frac{3 \\\\pi}{2}}{\\\\int }_{0}^{- 4 \\\\cos{\\\\left(\\\\theta \\\\right)}}{\\\\int }_{-6}^{r \\\\cos{\\\\left(\\\\theta \\\\right)} + 5}\\\\frac{r}{\\\\sqrt{r^{2} + 2}}\\\\,dz\\\\,\\\\text{d}r\\\\,\\\\text{d}\\\\theta \" :db/id 17592186537013} {:answer/id #uuid \"51c76675-9e2c-59b9-8577-4dc096a8cdfb\" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value \"{\\\\int }_{\\\\frac{\\\\pi}{2}}^{\\\\frac{3 \\\\pi}{2}}{\\\\int }_{0}^{- 8 \\\\cos{\\\\left(\\\\theta \\\\right)}}{\\\\int }_{r \\\\cos{\\\\left(\\\\theta \\\\right)} + 5}^{-6}\\\\frac{r}{\\\\sqrt{r^{2} + 2}}\\\\,dz\\\\,\\\\text{d}r\\\\,\\\\text{d}\\\\theta \" :db/id 17592186537016} {:answer/id #uuid \"5d3b8ee2-e524-5b41-8574-e188f91ae2b5\" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value \"{\\\\int }_{\\\\frac{\\\\pi}{2}}^{\\\\frac{3 \\\\pi}{2}}{\\\\int }_{0}^{- 8 \\\\cos{\\\\left(\\\\theta \\\\right)}}{\\\\int }_{-6}^{r \\\\cos{\\\\left(\\\\theta \\\\right)} + 5}\\\\frac{r^{2}}{\\\\sqrt{r^{2} + 2}}\\\\,dz\\\\,\\\\text{d}r\\\\,\\\\text{d}\\\\theta \" :db/id 17592186537020} {:answer/id #uuid \"a36208fb-a2e1-572d-a6a9-8b8ccf4ee98c\" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value \"{\\\\int }_{\\\\frac{\\\\pi}{2}}^{\\\\frac{3 \\\\pi}{2}}{\\\\int }_{0}^{- 8 \\\\cos{\\\\left(\\\\theta \\\\right)}}{\\\\int }_{-6}^{r \\\\cos{\\\\left(\\\\theta \\\\right)} + 5}\\\\frac{r}{\\\\sqrt{r^{2} + 2}}\\\\,dz\\\\,\\\\text{d}r\\\\,\\\\text{d}\\\\theta \" :db/id 17592186537045} {:answer/id #uuid \"d3040689-2239-57e5-8500-1ac134938960\" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value \"{\\\\int }_{\\\\frac{\\\\pi}{2}}^{\\\\frac{3 \\\\pi}{2}}{\\\\int }_{0}^{- 8 \\\\cos{\\\\left(\\\\theta \\\\right)}}{\\\\int }_{-6}^{r \\\\cos{\\\\left(\\\\theta \\\\right)} + 5}\\\\frac{1}{\\\\sqrt{r^{2} + 2}}\\\\,dz\\\\,\\\\text{d}r\\\\,\\\\text{d}\\\\theta \" :db/id 17592186537062}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid \"a36208fb-a2e1-572d-a6a9-8b8ccf4ee98c\" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value \"{\\\\int }_{\\\\frac{\\\\pi}{2}}^{\\\\frac{3 \\\\pi}{2}}{\\\\int }_{0}^{- 8 \\\\cos{\\\\left(\\\\theta \\\\right)}}{\\\\int }_{-6}^{r \\\\cos{\\\\left(\\\\theta \\\\right)} + 5}\\\\frac{r}{\\\\sqrt{r^{2} + 2}}\\\\,dz\\\\,\\\\text{d}r\\\\,\\\\text{d}\\\\theta \" :db/id 17592186537045} :answer-field/id #uuid \"3c179be8-68d6-5589-9fd5-03692660f907\" :answer-field/key \"selection\" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186537009}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid \"a4df6a09-850b-5ef6-8bf5-1f79b92be5fa\" :question/problem \"If the solid region $R$ is given by \\n\\n$$\\nR=\\\\{(x,y,z)\\\\,:\\\\,(x+4)^{2}+{y}^{2}\\\\le 16,\\\\,-6\\\\le z\\\\le x + 5\\\\},\\n$$\\n\\nthen express the triple integral\\n\\n$$\\n{∭}_{R}\\\\frac{1}{\\\\sqrt{x^{2} + y^{2} + 2}}\\\\,\\\\text{d}V\\n$$\\n\\nas an equivalent integral in cylindrical polar coordinates.\" :question/worked-solution \"We will use the change of variables formula in the form\\n\\n$$\\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\\\,dV={∭}_{Δ}\\\\,f(r\\\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\\\theta ,r\\\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\\\theta ,z)\\\\,r\\\\,dz\\\\,dr\\\\,d\\\\theta .\\n$$\\n\\nIf $(r,\\\\theta ,z)$ are the cylindrical polar coordinates of a point, then its Cartesian coordinates $(x,y,z)$ can be found by using the formulas\\n\\n$$\\nx=r\\\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\\\theta ,\\\\,y=r\\\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\\\theta ,\\\\,z=z.\\n$$\\n\\nFirst, let's sketch the projection of $R$ onto the $xy$-plane:\\n\\n![](images/8b/8b16f7e69a4ce84b0b7c4597defd9e9f55593e50623f344f001d24518d59c483.png)\\n\\nThe region $R$ is a right vertical cylinder of radius $4$ whose axis passes through the point $(-4,0)$ in the $xy$-plane, and is\\n\\n- bounded below by $z=-6,$ and\\n\\n- bounded above by $z=x + 5=r \\\\cos{\\\\left(\\\\theta \\\\right)} + 5.$\\n\\nWith that in mind, let's write the equation $(x+4)^{2}+{y}^{2}=16$ in polar coordinates:\\n\\n$$\\n\\\\begin{aligned}(x+4)^{2}+{y}^{2} & =16 \\\\\\\\ (r\\\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\\\theta+4)^{2}+(r\\\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\\\theta)^{2} & =16 \\\\\\\\ {r}^{2}{cos}^{2}⁡\\\\theta +8r\\\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\\\theta+16+{r}^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡\\\\theta & =16 \\\\\\\\ {r}^{2}({cos}^{2}⁡\\\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\\\theta )+8r\\\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\\\theta & =0 \\\\\\\\ {r}^{2}+8r\\\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\\\theta & =0 \\\\\\\\ r(r+8\\\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\\\theta) & =0\\\\end{aligned}\\n$$\\n\\nSince $r$ cannot be identical to zero on the entire circle, we must have $r=- 8 \\\\cos{\\\\left(\\\\theta \\\\right)}.$\\n\\nAs a result, in cylindrical polar coordinates, our region can be expressed as \\n\\n$$\\nΔ=\\\\{(r,\\\\theta ,z)\\\\,:\\\\,0\\\\le r\\\\le - 8 \\\\cos{\\\\left(\\\\theta \\\\right)},\\\\,\\\\frac{\\\\pi}{2}\\\\le \\\\theta \\\\le \\\\frac{3 \\\\pi}{2},\\\\,-6\\\\le z\\\\le r \\\\cos{\\\\left(\\\\theta \\\\right)} + 5\\\\}.\\n$$\\n\\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain  \\n\\n$$\\n\\\\begin{aligned}{∭}_{R}\\\\frac{1}{\\\\sqrt{x^{2} + y^{2} + 2}}\\\\,\\\\text{d}V & ={∭}_{Δ}\\\\frac{1}{\\\\sqrt{(r\\\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\\\theta)^{2}+(r\\\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\\\theta)^{2}+2}}\\\\cdot r\\\\,dz\\\\,dr\\\\,d\\\\theta \\\\\\\\  & ={∭}_{Δ}\\\\frac{r}{\\\\sqrt{{r}^{2}({cos}^{2}⁡\\\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\\\theta )+2}}\\\\,dz\\\\,dr\\\\,d\\\\theta \\\\\\\\  & ={\\\\int }_{\\\\frac{\\\\pi}{2}}^{\\\\frac{3 \\\\pi}{2}}{\\\\int }_{0}^{- 8 \\\\cos{\\\\left(\\\\theta \\\\right)}}{\\\\int }_{-6}^{r \\\\cos{\\\\left(\\\\theta \\\\right)} + 5}\\\\frac{r}{\\\\sqrt{r^{2} + 2}}\\\\,dz\\\\,\\\\text{d}r\\\\,\\\\text{d}\\\\theta .\\\\end{aligned}\\n$$\"} {:db/id 17592186537048 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid \"098d3517-e148-57fb-b802-0b2dbc349278\" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value \"{\\\\int }_{0}^{\\\\frac{\\\\pi}{2}}{\\\\int }_{0}^{8 \\\\sin{\\\\left(\\\\theta \\\\right)}}{\\\\int }_{-2}^{r \\\\sin{\\\\left(\\\\theta \\\\right)}}r \\\\sqrt{r^{2} + 1}\\\\,dz\\\\,\\\\text{d}r\\\\,\\\\text{d}\\\\theta \" :db/id 17592186536985} {:answer/id #uuid \"24acc4ff-e0b0-5c78-95ce-b81732e7ce1a\" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value \"{\\\\int }_{0}^{\\\\frac{\\\\pi}{2}}{\\\\int }_{0}^{8 \\\\sin{\\\\left(\\\\theta \\\\right)}}{\\\\int }_{-2}^{r \\\\cos{\\\\left(\\\\theta \\\\right)}}r \\\\sqrt{r^{2} + 1}\\\\,dz\\\\,\\\\text{d}r\\\\,\\\\text{d}\\\\theta \" :db/id 17592186536997} {:answer/id #uuid \"3554a9cb-6c59-5f65-87eb-c96e1206a180\" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value \"{\\\\int }_{0}^{\\\\frac{\\\\pi}{2}}{\\\\int }_{0}^{8 \\\\sin{\\\\left(\\\\theta \\\\right)}}{\\\\int }_{-2}^{r \\\\cos{\\\\left(\\\\theta \\\\right)}}\\\\sqrt{r^{2} + 1}\\\\,dz\\\\,\\\\text{d}r\\\\,\\\\text{d}\\\\theta \" :db/id 17592186537005} {:answer/id #uuid \"42573f2c-f0d7-5b0a-a697-3d745e18a680\" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value \"{\\\\int }_{0}^{\\\\pi}{\\\\int }_{0}^{8 \\\\sin{\\\\left(\\\\theta \\\\right)}}{\\\\int }_{-2}^{r \\\\cos{\\\\left(\\\\theta \\\\right)}}r \\\\sqrt{r^{2} + 1}\\\\,dz\\\\,\\\\text{d}r\\\\,\\\\text{d}\\\\theta \" :db/id 17592186537012} {:answer/id #uuid \"b2ef5227-2542-5600-9c83-8758d74eee3e\" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value \"{\\\\int }_{0}^{\\\\pi}{\\\\int }_{0}^{8 \\\\sin{\\\\left(\\\\theta \\\\right)}}{\\\\int }_{-2}^{r \\\\sin{\\\\left(\\\\theta \\\\right)}}\\\\sqrt{r^{2} + 1}\\\\,dz\\\\,\\\\text{d}r\\\\,\\\\text{d}\\\\theta \" :db/id 17592186537050}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid \"24acc4ff-e0b0-5c78-95ce-b81732e7ce1a\" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value \"{\\\\int }_{0}^{\\\\frac{\\\\pi}{2}}{\\\\int }_{0}^{8 \\\\sin{\\\\left(\\\\theta \\\\right)}}{\\\\int }_{-2}^{r \\\\cos{\\\\left(\\\\theta \\\\right)}}r \\\\sqrt{r^{2} + 1}\\\\,dz\\\\,\\\\text{d}r\\\\,\\\\text{d}\\\\theta \" :db/id 17592186536997} :answer-field/id #uuid \"8f1ed8e4-2fa2-59d9-98df-3af8a6aba784\" :answer-field/key \"selection\" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186537037}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid \"ab4dd324-45c0-5df5-a923-9696a0511377\" :question/problem \"If the solid region $R$ is given by \\n\\n$$\\nR=\\\\{(x,y,z)\\\\,:\\\\,{x}^{2}+(y-4)^{2}\\\\le 16,x\\\\ge 0,\\\\,-2\\\\le z\\\\le x\\\\},\\n$$\\n\\nthen express the triple integral\\n\\n$$\\n{∭}_{R}\\\\sqrt{x^{2} + y^{2} + 1}\\\\,\\\\text{d}V\\n$$\\n\\nas an equivalent integral in cylindrical polar coordinates.\" :question/worked-solution \"We will use the change of variables formula in the form\\n\\n$$\\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\\\,dV={∭}_{Δ}\\\\,f(r\\\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\\\theta ,r\\\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\\\theta ,z)\\\\,r\\\\,dz\\\\,dr\\\\,d\\\\theta .\\n$$\\n\\nIf $(r,\\\\theta ,z)$ are the cylindrical polar coordinates of a point, then its Cartesian coordinates $(x,y,z)$ can be found by using the formulas\\n\\n$$\\nx=r\\\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\\\theta ,\\\\,y=r\\\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\\\theta ,\\\\,z=z.\\n$$\\n\\nFirst, let's sketch the projection of $R$ onto the $xy$-plane:\\n\\n![](images/26/262fdb09ab8f6ee5de1a31cec2d238cb633ae2d5dfa2e99d80ed58d4cb849ec5.png)\\n\\nThe region $R$ is one-half of a right vertical cylinder of radius $4$ whose axis passes through the point $(0,4)$ in the $xy$-plane, and is\\n\\n- bounded below by $z=-2,$ and\\n\\n- bounded above by $z=x=r \\\\cos{\\\\left(\\\\theta \\\\right)}.$\\n\\nWith that in mind, let's now write the ${x}^{2}+(y-4)^{2}=16$ in polar coordinates:\\n\\n$$\\n\\\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+(y-4)^{2} & =16 \\\\\\\\ (r\\\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\\\theta)^{2}+(r\\\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\\\theta-4)^{2} & =16 \\\\\\\\ {r}^{2}{cos}^{2}⁡\\\\theta +{r}^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡\\\\theta -8r\\\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\\\theta+16 & =16 \\\\\\\\ {r}^{2}({cos}^{2}⁡\\\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\\\theta )-8r\\\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\\\theta & =0 \\\\\\\\ {r}^{2}-8r\\\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\\\theta & =0 \\\\\\\\ r(r-8\\\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\\\theta) & =0\\\\end{aligned}\\n$$\\n\\nSince $r$ cannot be identical to zero on the entire semicircle, we must have $r=8 \\\\sin{\\\\left(\\\\theta \\\\right)}.$\\n\\nAs a result, in cylindrical polar coordinates, our region can be expressed as \\n\\n$$\\nΔ=\\\\{(r,\\\\theta ,z)\\\\,:\\\\,0\\\\le r\\\\le 8 \\\\sin{\\\\left(\\\\theta \\\\right)},\\\\,0\\\\le \\\\theta \\\\le \\\\frac{\\\\pi}{2},\\\\,-2\\\\le z\\\\le r \\\\cos{\\\\left(\\\\theta \\\\right)}\\\\}.\\n$$\\n\\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain\\n\\n$$\\n\\\\begin{aligned}{∭}_{R}\\\\sqrt{x^{2} + y^{2} + 1}\\\\,\\\\text{d}V & ={∭}_{Δ}\\\\sqrt{(r\\\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\\\theta)^{2}+(r\\\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\\\theta)^{2}+1}\\\\cdot r\\\\,dz\\\\,dr\\\\,d\\\\theta \\\\\\\\  & ={∭}_{Δ}\\\\sqrt{{r}^{2}({cos}^{2}⁡\\\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\\\theta )+1}\\\\cdot r\\\\,dz\\\\,dr\\\\,d\\\\theta \\\\\\\\  & ={\\\\int }_{0}^{\\\\frac{\\\\pi}{2}}{\\\\int }_{0}^{8 \\\\sin{\\\\left(\\\\theta \\\\right)}}{\\\\int }_{-2}^{r \\\\cos{\\\\left(\\\\theta \\\\right)}}r \\\\sqrt{r^{2} + 1}\\\\,dz\\\\,\\\\text{d}r\\\\,\\\\text{d}\\\\theta .\\\\end{aligned}\\n$$\"} {:db/id 17592186537052 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid \"0a83957d-072f-56dd-967f-e6e3cad6b479\" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value \"{\\\\int }_{0}^{\\\\frac{\\\\pi}{2}}{\\\\int }_{0}^{4 \\\\cos{\\\\left(\\\\theta \\\\right)}}{\\\\int }_{1}^{2}r^{3}\\\\,dz\\\\,\\\\text{d}r\\\\,\\\\text{d}\\\\theta \" :db/id 17592186536986} {:answer/id #uuid \"2156c48d-60f6-5a75-b7bd-532244308380\" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value \"{\\\\int }_{0}^{\\\\frac{\\\\pi}{2}}{\\\\int }_{0}^{8 \\\\cos{\\\\left(\\\\theta \\\\right)}}{\\\\int }_{1}^{2}r^{4}\\\\,dz\\\\,\\\\text{d}r\\\\,\\\\text{d}\\\\theta \" :db/id 17592186536995} {:answer/id #uuid \"55850eab-9b9d-5708-b164-b99babf0d7c1\" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value \"{\\\\int }_{0}^{\\\\frac{\\\\pi}{2}}{\\\\int }_{0}^{8 \\\\cos{\\\\left(\\\\theta \\\\right)}}{\\\\int }_{1}^{2}r^{3}\\\\,dz\\\\,\\\\text{d}r\\\\,\\\\text{d}\\\\theta \" :db/id 17592186537017} {:answer/id #uuid \"8d424d92-a14d-56fd-9646-621bf9980044\" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value \"{\\\\int }_{0}^{\\\\frac{\\\\pi}{2}}{\\\\int }_{0}^{8 \\\\cos{\\\\left(\\\\theta \\\\right)}}{\\\\int }_{2}^{1}r^{3}\\\\,dz\\\\,\\\\text{d}r\\\\,\\\\text{d}\\\\theta \" :db/id 17592186537036} {:answer/id #uuid \"d8b1f40f-3ec1-5b86-a168-1bf950f81dbc\" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value \"{\\\\int }_{0}^{\\\\frac{\\\\pi}{2}}{\\\\int }_{0}^{8 \\\\cos{\\\\left(\\\\theta \\\\right)}}{\\\\int }_{1}^{2}r^{2}\\\\,dz\\\\,\\\\text{d}r\\\\,\\\\text{d}\\\\theta \" :db/id 17592186537065}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid \"55850eab-9b9d-5708-b164-b99babf0d7c1\" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value \"{\\\\int }_{0}^{\\\\frac{\\\\pi}{2}}{\\\\int }_{0}^{8 \\\\cos{\\\\left(\\\\theta \\\\right)}}{\\\\int }_{1}^{2}r^{3}\\\\,dz\\\\,\\\\text{d}r\\\\,\\\\text{d}\\\\theta \" :db/id 17592186537017} :answer-field/id #uuid \"3c67f654-3428-5c4c-b065-5f85356ec766\" :answer-field/key \"selection\" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186537010}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid \"b5e1ca18-2705-5bdb-9505-7d24e5965b22\" :question/problem \"If the solid region $R$ is given by \\n\\n$$\\nR=\\\\{(x,y,z)\\\\,:\\\\,(x-4)^{2}+{y}^{2}\\\\le 16,y\\\\ge 0,\\\\,1\\\\le z\\\\le 2\\\\},\\n$$\\n\\nthen express the triple integral\\n\\n$$\\n{∭}_{R}x^{2} + y^{2}\\\\,\\\\text{d}V\\n$$\\n\\nas an equivalent integral in cylindrical polar coordinates.\" :question/worked-solution \"We will use the change of variables formula in the form\\n\\n$$\\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\\\,dV={∭}_{Δ}\\\\,f(r\\\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\\\theta ,r\\\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\\\theta ,z)\\\\,r\\\\,dz\\\\,dr\\\\,d\\\\theta .\\n$$\\n\\nIf $(r,\\\\theta ,z)$ are the cylindrical polar coordinates of a point, then its Cartesian coordinates $(x,y,z)$ can be found by using the formulas\\n\\n$$\\nx=r\\\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\\\theta ,\\\\,y=r\\\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\\\theta ,\\\\,z=z.\\n$$\\n\\nFirst, let's sketch the projection of $R$ onto the $xy$-plane:\\n\\n![](images/80/80a919b28ae7ed559b06b34bd8512859794a7d6550970b56347e2deb653bf76d.png)\\n\\nThe region $R$ is one-half of a right vertical cylinder of radius $4$ whose axis passes through the point $(4,0)$ in the $xy$-plane, and is\\n\\n- bounded below by $z=1,$ and\\n\\n- bounded above by $z=2.$\\n\\nWith that in mind, let's now write the $(x-4)^{2}+{y}^{2}=16$ in polar coordinates:\\n\\n$$\\n\\\\begin{aligned}(x-4)^{2}+{y}^{2} & =16 \\\\\\\\ (r\\\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\\\theta-4)^{2}+(r\\\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\\\theta)^{2} & =16 \\\\\\\\ {r}^{2}{cos}^{2}⁡\\\\theta -8r\\\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\\\theta+16+{r}^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡\\\\theta & =16 \\\\\\\\ {r}^{2}({cos}^{2}⁡\\\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\\\theta )-8r\\\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\\\theta & =0 \\\\\\\\ {r}^{2}-8r\\\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\\\theta & =0 \\\\\\\\ r(r-8\\\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\\\theta) & =0\\\\end{aligned}\\n$$\\n\\nSince $r$ cannot be identical to zero on the entire semicircle, we must have $r=8 \\\\cos{\\\\left(\\\\theta \\\\right)}.$\\n\\nAs a result, in cylindrical polar coordinates, our region can be expressed as \\n\\n$$\\nΔ=\\\\{(r,\\\\theta ,z)\\\\,:\\\\,0\\\\le r\\\\le 8 \\\\cos{\\\\left(\\\\theta \\\\right)},\\\\,0\\\\le \\\\theta \\\\le \\\\frac{\\\\pi}{2},\\\\,1\\\\le z\\\\le 2\\\\}.\\n$$\\n\\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain\\n\\n$$\\n\\\\begin{aligned}{∭}_{R}x^{2} + y^{2}\\\\,\\\\text{d}V & ={∭}_{Δ}((r\\\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\\\theta)^{2}+(r\\\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\\\theta)^{2})\\\\cdot r\\\\,dz\\\\,dr\\\\,d\\\\theta \\\\\\\\  & ={∭}_{Δ}{r}^{2}({cos}^{2}⁡\\\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\\\theta )\\\\cdot r\\\\,dz\\\\,dr\\\\,d\\\\theta \\\\\\\\  & ={\\\\int }_{0}^{\\\\frac{\\\\pi}{2}}{\\\\int }_{0}^{8 \\\\cos{\\\\left(\\\\theta \\\\right)}}{\\\\int }_{1}^{2}r^{3}\\\\,dz\\\\,\\\\text{d}r\\\\,\\\\text{d}\\\\theta .\\\\end{aligned}\\n$$\"} {:db/id 17592186537058 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid \"15f5725f-873a-5cb2-9d93-8dd091bb64fc\" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value \"{\\\\int }_{\\\\frac{3 \\\\pi}{2}}^{2 \\\\pi}{\\\\int }_{0}^{8 \\\\cos{\\\\left(\\\\theta \\\\right)}}{\\\\int }_{4}^{r \\\\sin{\\\\left(\\\\theta \\\\right)}}\\\\frac{r}{\\\\sqrt{z + 5}}\\\\,dz\\\\,\\\\text{d}r\\\\,\\\\text{d}\\\\theta \" :db/id 17592186536989} {:answer/id #uuid \"341da02c-a537-571e-98d0-36040cb4c3d3\" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value \"{\\\\int }_{\\\\frac{3 \\\\pi}{2}}^{2 \\\\pi}{\\\\int }_{0}^{8 \\\\cos{\\\\left(\\\\theta \\\\right)}}{\\\\int }_{r \\\\sin{\\\\left(\\\\theta \\\\right)}}^{4}\\\\frac{r}{\\\\sqrt{z + 5}}\\\\,dz\\\\,\\\\text{d}r\\\\,\\\\text{d}\\\\theta \" :db/id 17592186537002} {:answer/id #uuid \"55f37382-115f-5176-b367-f0f7693481cc\" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value \"{\\\\int }_{\\\\frac{3 \\\\pi}{2}}^{2 \\\\pi}{\\\\int }_{0}^{8 \\\\cos{\\\\left(\\\\theta \\\\right)}}{\\\\int }_{r \\\\sin{\\\\left(\\\\theta \\\\right)}}^{4}\\\\frac{1}{\\\\sqrt{z + 5}}\\\\,dz\\\\,\\\\text{d}r\\\\,\\\\text{d}\\\\theta \" :db/id 17592186537018} {:answer/id #uuid \"95c46129-e371-5472-a8c0-39ca8937604f\" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value \"{\\\\int }_{\\\\frac{3 \\\\pi}{2}}^{2 \\\\pi}{\\\\int }_{0}^{4 \\\\cos{\\\\left(\\\\theta \\\\right)}}{\\\\int }_{r \\\\sin{\\\\left(\\\\theta \\\\right)}}^{4}\\\\frac{r}{\\\\sqrt{z + 5}}\\\\,dz\\\\,\\\\text{d}r\\\\,\\\\text{d}\\\\theta \" :db/id 17592186537038} {:answer/id #uuid \"dede804f-1f13-5287-811a-67eb0c9566f2\" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value \"{\\\\int }_{\\\\frac{3 \\\\pi}{2}}^{2 \\\\pi}{\\\\int }_{0}^{8 \\\\cos{\\\\left(\\\\theta \\\\right)}}{\\\\int }_{r \\\\sin{\\\\left(\\\\theta \\\\right)}}^{4}\\\\frac{r^{2}}{\\\\sqrt{z + 5}}\\\\,dz\\\\,\\\\text{d}r\\\\,\\\\text{d}\\\\theta \" :db/id 17592186537067}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid \"341da02c-a537-571e-98d0-36040cb4c3d3\" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value \"{\\\\int }_{\\\\frac{3 \\\\pi}{2}}^{2 \\\\pi}{\\\\int }_{0}^{8 \\\\cos{\\\\left(\\\\theta \\\\right)}}{\\\\int }_{r \\\\sin{\\\\left(\\\\theta \\\\right)}}^{4}\\\\frac{r}{\\\\sqrt{z + 5}}\\\\,dz\\\\,\\\\text{d}r\\\\,\\\\text{d}\\\\theta \" :db/id 17592186537002} :answer-field/id #uuid \"f3833d31-d0a7-5f2a-ba09-40678b399d56\" :answer-field/key \"selection\" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186537074}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid \"c80df8b3-485c-5c4b-a01f-65b076ecb41f\" :question/problem \"If the solid region $R$ is given by \\n\\n$$\\nR=\\\\{(x,y,z)\\\\,:\\\\,(x-4)^{2}+{y}^{2}\\\\le 16,y\\\\le 0,\\\\,y\\\\le z\\\\le 4\\\\},\\n$$\\n\\nthen express the triple integral\\n\\n$$\\n{∭}_{R}\\\\frac{1}{\\\\sqrt{z + 5}}\\\\,\\\\text{d}V\\n$$\\n\\nas an equivalent integral in cylindrical polar coordinates.\" :question/worked-solution \"We will use the change of variables formula in the form\\n\\n$$\\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\\\,dV={∭}_{Δ}\\\\,f(r\\\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\\\theta ,r\\\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\\\theta ,z)\\\\,r\\\\,dz\\\\,dr\\\\,d\\\\theta .\\n$$\\n\\nIf $(r,\\\\theta ,z)$ are the cylindrical polar coordinates of a point, then its Cartesian coordinates $(x,y,z)$ can be found by using the formulas\\n\\n$$\\nx=r\\\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\\\theta ,\\\\,y=r\\\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\\\theta ,\\\\,z=z.\\n$$\\n\\nFirst, let's sketch the projection of $R$ onto the $xy$-plane:\\n\\n![](images/51/510a5ecd48459be9372c1a797d00cc78a167b7ebc0563063e4f1a1e187e32bd0.png)\\n\\nThe region $R$ is one-half of a right vertical cylinder of radius $4$ whose axis passes through the point $(4,0)$ in the $xy$-plane, and is\\n\\n- bounded below by $z=y=r \\\\sin{\\\\left(\\\\theta \\\\right)},$ and\\n\\n- bounded above by $z=4.$\\n\\nWith that in mind, let's now write the $(x-4)^{2}+{y}^{2}=16$ in polar coordinates:\\n\\n$$\\n\\\\begin{aligned}(x-4)^{2}+{y}^{2} & =16 \\\\\\\\ (r\\\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\\\theta-4)^{2}+(r\\\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\\\theta)^{2} & =16 \\\\\\\\ {r}^{2}{cos}^{2}⁡\\\\theta -8r\\\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\\\theta+16+{r}^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡\\\\theta & =16 \\\\\\\\ {r}^{2}({cos}^{2}⁡\\\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\\\theta )-8r\\\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\\\theta & =0 \\\\\\\\ {r}^{2}-8r\\\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\\\theta & =0 \\\\\\\\ r(r-8\\\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\\\theta) & =0\\\\end{aligned}\\n$$\\n\\nSince $r$ cannot be identical to zero on the entire semicircle, we must have $r=8 \\\\cos{\\\\left(\\\\theta \\\\right)}.$\\n\\nAs a result, in cylindrical polar coordinates, our region can be expressed as \\n\\n$$\\nΔ=\\\\{(r,\\\\theta ,z)\\\\,:\\\\,0\\\\le r\\\\le 8 \\\\cos{\\\\left(\\\\theta \\\\right)},\\\\,\\\\frac{3 \\\\pi}{2}\\\\le \\\\theta \\\\le 2 \\\\pi,\\\\,r \\\\sin{\\\\left(\\\\theta \\\\right)}\\\\le z\\\\le 4\\\\}.\\n$$\\n\\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain  \\n\\n$$\\n\\\\begin{aligned}{∭}_{R}\\\\frac{1}{\\\\sqrt{z + 5}}\\\\,\\\\text{d}V & ={∭}_{Δ}\\\\frac{1}{\\\\sqrt{z + 5}}\\\\cdot r\\\\,dz\\\\,dr\\\\,d\\\\theta \\\\\\\\  & ={\\\\int }_{\\\\frac{3 \\\\pi}{2}}^{2 \\\\pi}{\\\\int }_{0}^{8 \\\\cos{\\\\left(\\\\theta \\\\right)}}{\\\\int }_{r \\\\sin{\\\\left(\\\\theta \\\\right)}}^{4}\\\\frac{r}{\\\\sqrt{z + 5}}\\\\,dz\\\\,\\\\text{d}r\\\\,\\\\text{d}\\\\theta .\\\\end{aligned}\\n$$\"} {:db/id 17592186537064 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid \"6797e6ea-0e50-5bcf-8670-1502efade88b\" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value \"{\\\\int }_{0}^{\\\\frac{\\\\pi}{2}}{\\\\int }_{0}^{2 \\\\cos{\\\\left(\\\\theta \\\\right)}}{\\\\int }_{4}^{6}r^{3}\\\\,dz\\\\,\\\\text{d}r\\\\,\\\\text{d}\\\\theta \" :db/id 17592186537023} {:answer/id #uuid \"6a1f36d1-2f5f-5b5d-b048-0f5b93961b12\" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value \"{\\\\int }_{0}^{\\\\frac{\\\\pi}{2}}{\\\\int }_{0}^{4 \\\\cos{\\\\left(\\\\theta \\\\right)}}{\\\\int }_{6}^{4}r^{3}\\\\,dz\\\\,\\\\text{d}r\\\\,\\\\text{d}\\\\theta \" :db/id 17592186537026} {:answer/id #uuid \"80b41028-1697-5a18-89f0-2ed59ee0559f\" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value \"{\\\\int }_{0}^{\\\\frac{\\\\pi}{2}}{\\\\int }_{0}^{4 \\\\cos{\\\\left(\\\\theta \\\\right)}}{\\\\int }_{4}^{6}r^{2}\\\\,dz\\\\,\\\\text{d}r\\\\,\\\\text{d}\\\\theta \" :db/id 17592186537034} {:answer/id #uuid \"c719c5f0-f401-5da6-91a4-572901eb262b\" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value \"{\\\\int }_{0}^{\\\\frac{\\\\pi}{2}}{\\\\int }_{0}^{4 \\\\cos{\\\\left(\\\\theta \\\\right)}}{\\\\int }_{4}^{6}r^{3}\\\\,dz\\\\,\\\\text{d}r\\\\,\\\\text{d}\\\\theta \" :db/id 17592186537057} {:answer/id #uuid \"fb13a566-d121-503a-8bc9-4bd42bb083df\" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value \"{\\\\int }_{0}^{\\\\frac{\\\\pi}{2}}{\\\\int }_{0}^{4 \\\\cos{\\\\left(\\\\theta \\\\right)}}{\\\\int }_{4}^{6}r^{4}\\\\,dz\\\\,\\\\text{d}r\\\\,\\\\text{d}\\\\theta \" :db/id 17592186537077}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid \"c719c5f0-f401-5da6-91a4-572901eb262b\" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value \"{\\\\int }_{0}^{\\\\frac{\\\\pi}{2}}{\\\\int }_{0}^{4 \\\\cos{\\\\left(\\\\theta \\\\right)}}{\\\\int }_{4}^{6}r^{3}\\\\,dz\\\\,\\\\text{d}r\\\\,\\\\text{d}\\\\theta \" :db/id 17592186537057} :answer-field/id #uuid \"5d4af9d5-325d-5634-94af-ef6d75847f12\" :answer-field/key \"selection\" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186537021}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid \"d488f663-9171-597d-b16b-2f0b7032086a\" :question/problem \"If the solid region $R$ is given by \\n\\n$$\\nR=\\\\{(x,y,z)\\\\,:\\\\,(x-2)^{2}+{y}^{2}\\\\le 4,y\\\\ge 0,\\\\,4\\\\le z\\\\le 6\\\\},\\n$$\\n\\nthen express the triple integral\\n\\n$$\\n{∭}_{R}x^{2} + y^{2}\\\\,\\\\text{d}V\\n$$\\n\\nas an equivalent integral in cylindrical polar coordinates.\" :question/worked-solution \"We will use the change of variables formula in the form\\n\\n$$\\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\\\,dV={∭}_{Δ}\\\\,f(r\\\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\\\theta ,r\\\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\\\theta ,z)\\\\,r\\\\,dz\\\\,dr\\\\,d\\\\theta .\\n$$\\n\\nIf $(r,\\\\theta ,z)$ are the cylindrical polar coordinates of a point, then its Cartesian coordinates $(x,y,z)$ can be found by using the formulas\\n\\n$$\\nx=r\\\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\\\theta ,\\\\,y=r\\\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\\\theta ,\\\\,z=z.\\n$$\\n\\nFirst, let's sketch the projection of $R$ onto the $xy$-plane:\\n\\n![](images/d2/d2b3344b7b910f5685d4fd81b75a600608af6b008778ff608cb084edb5a92da9.png)\\n\\nThe region $R$ is one-half of a right vertical cylinder of radius $2$ whose axis passes through the point $(2,0)$ in the $xy$-plane, and is\\n\\n- bounded below by $z=4,$ and\\n\\n- bounded above by $z=6.$\\n\\nWith that in mind, let's now write the $(x-2)^{2}+{y}^{2}=4$ in polar coordinates:\\n\\n$$\\n\\\\begin{aligned}(x-2)^{2}+{y}^{2} & =4 \\\\\\\\ (r\\\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\\\theta-2)^{2}+(r\\\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\\\theta)^{2} & =4 \\\\\\\\ {r}^{2}{cos}^{2}⁡\\\\theta -4r\\\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\\\theta+4+{r}^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡\\\\theta & =4 \\\\\\\\ {r}^{2}({cos}^{2}⁡\\\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\\\theta )-4r\\\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\\\theta & =0 \\\\\\\\ {r}^{2}-4r\\\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\\\theta & =0 \\\\\\\\ r(r-4\\\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\\\theta) & =0\\\\end{aligned}\\n$$\\n\\nSince $r$ cannot be identical to zero on the entire semicircle, we must have $r=4 \\\\cos{\\\\left(\\\\theta \\\\right)}.$\\n\\nAs a result, in cylindrical polar coordinates, our region can be expressed as \\n\\n$$\\nΔ=\\\\{(r,\\\\theta ,z)\\\\,:\\\\,0\\\\le r\\\\le 4 \\\\cos{\\\\left(\\\\theta \\\\right)},\\\\,0\\\\le \\\\theta \\\\le \\\\frac{\\\\pi}{2},\\\\,4\\\\le z\\\\le 6\\\\}.\\n$$\\n\\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain\\n\\n$$\\n\\\\begin{aligned}{∭}_{R}x^{2} + y^{2}\\\\,\\\\text{d}V & ={∭}_{Δ}((r\\\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\\\theta)^{2}+(r\\\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\\\theta)^{2})\\\\cdot r\\\\,dz\\\\,dr\\\\,d\\\\theta \\\\\\\\  & ={∭}_{Δ}{r}^{2}({cos}^{2}⁡\\\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\\\theta )\\\\cdot r\\\\,dz\\\\,dr\\\\,d\\\\theta \\\\\\\\  & ={\\\\int }_{0}^{\\\\frac{\\\\pi}{2}}{\\\\int }_{0}^{4 \\\\cos{\\\\left(\\\\theta \\\\right)}}{\\\\int }_{4}^{6}r^{3}\\\\,dz\\\\,\\\\text{d}r\\\\,\\\\text{d}\\\\theta .\\\\end{aligned}\\n$$\"} {:db/id 17592186537066 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid \"682a228b-b79d-5fcb-b15e-e67fdbaa8be7\" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value \"{\\\\int }_{\\\\frac{3 \\\\pi}{2}}^{2 \\\\pi}{\\\\int }_{0}^{4 \\\\cos{\\\\left(\\\\theta \\\\right)}}{\\\\int }_{r \\\\sin{\\\\left(\\\\theta \\\\right)}}^{10}\\\\frac{1}{\\\\sqrt{z + 5}}\\\\,dz\\\\,\\\\text{d}r\\\\,\\\\text{d}\\\\theta \" :db/id 17592186537024} {:answer/id #uuid \"6af500dd-3fdb-56d1-8617-670cec01eb55\" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value \"{\\\\int }_{\\\\frac{3 \\\\pi}{2}}^{2 \\\\pi}{\\\\int }_{0}^{4 \\\\cos{\\\\left(\\\\theta \\\\right)}}{\\\\int }_{r \\\\sin{\\\\left(\\\\theta \\\\right)}}^{10}\\\\frac{r^{2}}{\\\\sqrt{z + 5}}\\\\,dz\\\\,\\\\text{d}r\\\\,\\\\text{d}\\\\theta \" :db/id 17592186537028} {:answer/id #uuid \"7301b34c-817c-56a3-8683-9aaaf6122853\" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value \"{\\\\int }_{\\\\frac{3 \\\\pi}{2}}^{2 \\\\pi}{\\\\int }_{0}^{4 \\\\cos{\\\\left(\\\\theta \\\\right)}}{\\\\int }_{r \\\\sin{\\\\left(\\\\theta \\\\right)}}^{10}\\\\frac{r}{\\\\sqrt{z + 5}}\\\\,dz\\\\,\\\\text{d}r\\\\,\\\\text{d}\\\\theta \" :db/id 17592186537032} {:answer/id #uuid \"e0cad40a-69b5-528c-86d1-d835cef665eb\" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value \"{\\\\int }_{\\\\frac{3 \\\\pi}{2}}^{2 \\\\pi}{\\\\int }_{0}^{2 \\\\cos{\\\\left(\\\\theta \\\\right)}}{\\\\int }_{r \\\\sin{\\\\left(\\\\theta \\\\right)}}^{10}\\\\frac{r}{\\\\sqrt{z + 5}}\\\\,dz\\\\,\\\\text{d}r\\\\,\\\\text{d}\\\\theta \" :db/id 17592186537069} {:answer/id #uuid \"e796db0a-5eac-585e-902e-dc1ce488d7e5\" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value \"{\\\\int }_{\\\\frac{3 \\\\pi}{2}}^{2 \\\\pi}{\\\\int }_{0}^{4 \\\\cos{\\\\left(\\\\theta \\\\right)}}{\\\\int }_{10}^{r \\\\sin{\\\\left(\\\\theta \\\\right)}}\\\\frac{r}{\\\\sqrt{z + 5}}\\\\,dz\\\\,\\\\text{d}r\\\\,\\\\text{d}\\\\theta \" :db/id 17592186537071}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid \"7301b34c-817c-56a3-8683-9aaaf6122853\" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value \"{\\\\int }_{\\\\frac{3 \\\\pi}{2}}^{2 \\\\pi}{\\\\int }_{0}^{4 \\\\cos{\\\\left(\\\\theta \\\\right)}}{\\\\int }_{r \\\\sin{\\\\left(\\\\theta \\\\right)}}^{10}\\\\frac{r}{\\\\sqrt{z + 5}}\\\\,dz\\\\,\\\\text{d}r\\\\,\\\\text{d}\\\\theta \" :db/id 17592186537032} :answer-field/id #uuid \"c2c84f22-679b-57d9-9b4d-6d3035a36e20\" :answer-field/key \"selection\" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186537055}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid \"de103878-75c8-5f73-9ce2-41a2334b4eac\" :question/problem \"If the solid region $R$ is given by \\n\\n$$\\nR=\\\\{(x,y,z)\\\\,:\\\\,(x-2)^{2}+{y}^{2}\\\\le 4,y\\\\le 0,\\\\,y\\\\le z\\\\le 10\\\\},\\n$$\\n\\nthen express the triple integral\\n\\n$$\\n{∭}_{R}\\\\frac{1}{\\\\sqrt{z + 5}}\\\\,\\\\text{d}V\\n$$\\n\\nas an equivalent integral in cylindrical polar coordinates.\" :question/worked-solution \"We will use the change of variables formula in the form\\n\\n$$\\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\\\,dV={∭}_{Δ}\\\\,f(r\\\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\\\theta ,r\\\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\\\theta ,z)\\\\,r\\\\,dz\\\\,dr\\\\,d\\\\theta .\\n$$\\n\\nIf $(r,\\\\theta ,z)$ are the cylindrical polar coordinates of a point, then its Cartesian coordinates $(x,y,z)$ can be found by using the formulas\\n\\n$$\\nx=r\\\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\\\theta ,\\\\,y=r\\\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\\\theta ,\\\\,z=z.\\n$$\\n\\nFirst, let's sketch the projection of $R$ onto the $xy$-plane:\\n\\n![](images/d8/d8d5ba5f13ed464fa4e2b53fb2cd4fbdab92b3de371d3f7b535aeac8d0adb6d5.png)\\n\\nThe region $R$ is one-half of a right vertical cylinder of radius $2$ whose axis passes through the point $(2,0)$ in the $xy$-plane, and is\\n\\n- bounded below by $z=y=r \\\\sin{\\\\left(\\\\theta \\\\right)},$ and\\n\\n- bounded above by $z=10.$\\n\\nWith that in mind, let's now write the $(x-2)^{2}+{y}^{2}=4$ in polar coordinates:\\n\\n$$\\n\\\\begin{aligned}(x-2)^{2}+{y}^{2} & =4 \\\\\\\\ (r\\\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\\\theta-2)^{2}+(r\\\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\\\theta)^{2} & =4 \\\\\\\\ {r}^{2}{cos}^{2}⁡\\\\theta -4r\\\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\\\theta+4+{r}^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡\\\\theta & =4 \\\\\\\\ {r}^{2}({cos}^{2}⁡\\\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\\\theta )-4r\\\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\\\theta & =0 \\\\\\\\ {r}^{2}-4r\\\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\\\theta & =0 \\\\\\\\ r(r-4\\\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\\\theta) & =0\\\\end{aligned}\\n$$\\n\\nSince $r$ cannot be identical to zero on the entire semicircle, we must have $r=4 \\\\cos{\\\\left(\\\\theta \\\\right)}.$\\n\\nAs a result, in cylindrical polar coordinates, our region can be expressed as \\n\\n$$\\nΔ=\\\\{(r,\\\\theta ,z)\\\\,:\\\\,0\\\\le r\\\\le 4 \\\\cos{\\\\left(\\\\theta \\\\right)},\\\\,\\\\frac{3 \\\\pi}{2}\\\\le \\\\theta \\\\le 2 \\\\pi,\\\\,r \\\\sin{\\\\left(\\\\theta \\\\right)}\\\\le z\\\\le 10\\\\}.\\n$$\\n\\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain  \\n\\n$$\\n\\\\begin{aligned}{∭}_{R}\\\\frac{1}{\\\\sqrt{z + 5}}\\\\,\\\\text{d}V & ={∭}_{Δ}\\\\frac{1}{\\\\sqrt{z + 5}}\\\\cdot r\\\\,dz\\\\,dr\\\\,d\\\\theta \\\\\\\\  & ={\\\\int }_{\\\\frac{3 \\\\pi}{2}}^{2 \\\\pi}{\\\\int }_{0}^{4 \\\\cos{\\\\left(\\\\theta \\\\right)}}{\\\\int }_{r \\\\sin{\\\\left(\\\\theta \\\\right)}}^{10}\\\\frac{r}{\\\\sqrt{z + 5}}\\\\,dz\\\\,\\\\text{d}r\\\\,\\\\text{d}\\\\theta .\\\\end{aligned}\\n$$\"} {:db/id 17592186537072 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid \"15fafcac-b57c-5a49-aadc-f7ea513877d4\" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value \"{\\\\int }_{\\\\pi}^{2 \\\\pi}{\\\\int }_{0}^{- 8 \\\\sin{\\\\left(\\\\theta \\\\right)}}{\\\\int }_{0}^{r^{2} + 2}r^{3} \\\\cos^{2}{\\\\left(\\\\theta \\\\right)}\\\\,dz\\\\,\\\\text{d}r\\\\,\\\\text{d}\\\\theta \" :db/id 17592186536990} {:answer/id #uuid \"208a838f-c0e2-54fe-b2e6-dc7e12747ff6\" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value \"{\\\\int }_{\\\\pi}^{2 \\\\pi}{\\\\int }_{0}^{- 8 \\\\sin{\\\\left(\\\\theta \\\\right)}}{\\\\int }_{0}^{r^{2} + 2}r^{3} \\\\sin{\\\\left(\\\\theta \\\\right)} \\\\cos{\\\\left(\\\\theta \\\\right)}\\\\,dz\\\\,\\\\text{d}r\\\\,\\\\text{d}\\\\theta \" :db/id 17592186536993} {:answer/id #uuid \"7084a39e-ec6e-5c74-b493-fb831dae5598\" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value \"{\\\\int }_{\\\\pi}^{2 \\\\pi}{\\\\int }_{0}^{- 8 \\\\sin{\\\\left(\\\\theta \\\\right)}}{\\\\int }_{0}^{r^{2} + 2}r^{3} \\\\sin^{2}{\\\\left(\\\\theta \\\\right)}\\\\,dz\\\\,\\\\text{d}r\\\\,\\\\text{d}\\\\theta \" :db/id 17592186537031} {:answer/id #uuid \"b8f22e85-6995-51aa-910a-ab0f4ae5632a\" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value \"{\\\\int }_{\\\\pi}^{2 \\\\pi}{\\\\int }_{0}^{- 8 \\\\sin{\\\\left(\\\\theta \\\\right)}}{\\\\int }_{0}^{r^{2} + 2}r^{2} \\\\sin^{2}{\\\\left(\\\\theta \\\\right)}\\\\,dz\\\\,\\\\text{d}r\\\\,\\\\text{d}\\\\theta \" :db/id 17592186537054} {:answer/id #uuid \"fbd1b677-e296-592e-b708-0635f034aaf4\" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value \"{\\\\int }_{\\\\pi}^{2 \\\\pi}{\\\\int }_{0}^{- 8 \\\\sin{\\\\left(\\\\theta \\\\right)}}{\\\\int }_{0}^{r^{2} + 2}r^{2} \\\\cos^{2}{\\\\left(\\\\theta \\\\right)}\\\\,dz\\\\,\\\\text{d}r\\\\,\\\\text{d}\\\\theta \" :db/id 17592186537079}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid \"208a838f-c0e2-54fe-b2e6-dc7e12747ff6\" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value \"{\\\\int }_{\\\\pi}^{2 \\\\pi}{\\\\int }_{0}^{- 8 \\\\sin{\\\\left(\\\\theta \\\\right)}}{\\\\int }_{0}^{r^{2} + 2}r^{3} \\\\sin{\\\\left(\\\\theta \\\\right)} \\\\cos{\\\\left(\\\\theta \\\\right)}\\\\,dz\\\\,\\\\text{d}r\\\\,\\\\text{d}\\\\theta \" :db/id 17592186536993} :answer-field/id #uuid \"ac38c3ec-fa0a-5519-a3eb-cc2f31de45a8\" :answer-field/key \"selection\" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186537049}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid \"eea1a108-64c8-59f3-971b-a2f53cbd0dc5\" :question/problem \"If the solid region $R$ is given by \\n\\n$$\\nR=\\\\{(x,y,z)\\\\,:\\\\,{x}^{2}+(y+4)^{2}\\\\le 16,\\\\,0\\\\le z\\\\le x^{2} + y^{2} + 2\\\\},\\n$$\\n\\nthen express the triple integral\\n\\n$$\\n{∭}_{R}x y\\\\,\\\\text{d}V\\n$$\\n\\nas an equivalent integral in cylindrical polar coordinates.\" :question/worked-solution \"We will use the change of variables formula in the form\\n\\n$$\\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\\\,dV={∭}_{Δ}\\\\,f(r\\\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\\\theta ,r\\\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\\\theta ,z)\\\\,r\\\\,dz\\\\,dr\\\\,d\\\\theta .\\n$$\\n\\nIf $(r,\\\\theta ,z)$ are the cylindrical polar coordinates of a point, then its Cartesian coordinates $(x,y,z)$ can be found by using the formulas\\n\\n$$\\nx=r\\\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\\\theta ,\\\\,y=r\\\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\\\theta ,\\\\,z=z.\\n$$\\n\\nFirst, let's sketch the projection of $R$ onto the $xy$-plane:\\n\\n![](images/5e/5ed28b1e7b49276cdb59da432056597e5992b8c4015ac8e9384f6bfe3f1fb7ba.png)\\n\\nThe region $R$ is a right vertical cylinder of radius $4$ whose axis passes through the point $(0,-4)$ in the $xy$-plane, and is\\n\\n- bounded below by $z=0,$ and\\n\\n- bounded above by $z=x^{2} + y^{2} + 2=r^{2} + 2.$\\n\\nWith that in mind, let's write the equation ${x}^{2}+(y+4)^{2}=16$ in polar coordinates:\\n\\n$$\\n\\\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+(y+4)^{2} & =16 \\\\\\\\ (r\\\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\\\theta)^{2}+(r\\\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\\\theta+4)^{2} & =16 \\\\\\\\ {r}^{2}{cos}^{2}⁡\\\\theta +{r}^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡\\\\theta +8r\\\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\\\theta+16 & =16 \\\\\\\\ {r}^{2}({cos}^{2}⁡\\\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\\\theta )+8r\\\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\\\theta & =0 \\\\\\\\ {r}^{2}+8r\\\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\\\theta & =0 \\\\\\\\ r(r+8\\\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\\\theta) & =0\\\\end{aligned}\\n$$\\n\\nSince $r$ cannot be identical to zero on the entire circle, we must have $r=- 8 \\\\sin{\\\\left(\\\\theta \\\\right)}.$\\n\\nAs a result, in cylindrical polar coordinates, our region can be expressed as \\n\\n$$\\nΔ=\\\\{(r,\\\\theta ,z)\\\\,:\\\\,0\\\\le r\\\\le - 8 \\\\sin{\\\\left(\\\\theta \\\\right)},\\\\,\\\\pi\\\\le \\\\theta \\\\le 2 \\\\pi,\\\\,0\\\\le z\\\\le r^{2} + 2\\\\}.\\n$$\\n\\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain\\n\\n$$\\n\\\\begin{aligned}{∭}_{R}x y\\\\,\\\\text{d}V & ={∭}_{Δ}(r\\\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\\\theta)(r\\\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\\\theta)\\\\cdot r\\\\,dz\\\\,dr\\\\,d\\\\theta \\\\\\\\  & ={\\\\int }_{\\\\pi}^{2 \\\\pi}{\\\\int }_{0}^{- 8 \\\\sin{\\\\left(\\\\theta \\\\right)}}{\\\\int }_{0}^{r^{2} + 2}r^{3} \\\\sin{\\\\left(\\\\theta \\\\right)} \\\\cos{\\\\left(\\\\theta \\\\right)}\\\\,dz\\\\,\\\\text{d}r\\\\,\\\\text{d}\\\\theta .\\\\end{aligned}\\n$$\"} {:db/id 17592186555773 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid \"0d8fc843-895c-53f1-9b75-77dc01bfca72\" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value \"{\\\\int }_{-\\\\pi /2}^{\\\\pi /2}{\\\\int }_{0}^{4\\\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\\\theta }{\\\\int }_{r\\\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\\\theta -4}^{3}rz\\\\,dz\\\\,\\\\text{d}r\\\\,\\\\text{d}\\\\theta \" :db/id 17592186555026} {:answer/id #uuid \"24acc80e-45d9-597e-8b1f-ceca39e70c74\" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value \"{\\\\int }_{-\\\\pi /2}^{\\\\pi /2}{\\\\int }_{0}^{4\\\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\\\theta }{\\\\int }_{r\\\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\\\theta -4}^{3}{r}^{2}z\\\\,dz\\\\,\\\\text{d}r\\\\,\\\\text{d}\\\\theta \" :db/id 17592186555073} {:answer/id #uuid \"26cae292-83e2-5ef9-a6b6-284121035e4c\" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value \"{\\\\int }_{-\\\\pi /2}^{\\\\pi /2}{\\\\int }_{0}^{4\\\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\\\theta }{\\\\int }_{r\\\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\\\theta -4}^{3}rz\\\\,dz\\\\,\\\\text{d}r\\\\,\\\\text{d}\\\\theta \" :db/id 17592186555081} {:answer/id #uuid \"8465f297-b1de-54df-9fd9-0bae69bd3f2f\" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value \"{\\\\int }_{-\\\\pi /2}^{\\\\pi /2}{\\\\int }_{0}^{4\\\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\\\theta }{\\\\int }_{r\\\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\\\theta -4}^{3}rz\\\\,dz\\\\,\\\\text{d}r\\\\,\\\\text{d}\\\\theta \" :db/id 17592186555305} {:answer/id #uuid \"acea55ef-1d37-58bf-91f3-827f6515fd31\" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value \"{\\\\int }_{-\\\\pi /2}^{\\\\pi /2}{\\\\int }_{0}^{4\\\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\\\theta }{\\\\int }_{r\\\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\\\theta -4}^{3}z\\\\,dz\\\\,\\\\text{d}r\\\\,\\\\text{d}\\\\theta \" :db/id 17592186555416}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid \"8465f297-b1de-54df-9fd9-0bae69bd3f2f\" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value \"{\\\\int }_{-\\\\pi /2}^{\\\\pi /2}{\\\\int }_{0}^{4\\\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\\\theta }{\\\\int }_{r\\\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\\\theta -4}^{3}rz\\\\,dz\\\\,\\\\text{d}r\\\\,\\\\text{d}\\\\theta \" :db/id 17592186555305} :answer-field/id #uuid \"e8da3860-d059-5229-bff4-f91bfdfe7bd7\" :answer-field/key \"selection\" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186555750}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid \"22f18443-8c16-5b1a-8aa4-cdb902d79223\" :question/math-academy-id \"q-161628\" :question/problem \"If the solid region $R$ is given by \\n\\n$$\\nR=\\\\{(x,y,z)\\\\,:\\\\,(x-2)^{2}+{y}^{2}\\\\le 4,\\\\,y-4\\\\le z\\\\le 3\\\\},\\n$$\\n\\nthen $$\\n\\\\displaystyle {∭}_{R}z\\\\,\\\\text{d}V=\\n$$\" :question/worked-solution \"We will use the change of variables formula in the form\\n\\n$$\\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\\\,dV={∭}_{Δ}\\\\,f(r\\\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\\\theta ,r\\\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\\\theta ,z)\\\\,r\\\\,dz\\\\,dr\\\\,d\\\\theta .\\n$$\\n\\nIf $(r,\\\\theta ,z)$ are the cylindrical polar coordinates of a point, then its Cartesian coordinates $(x,y,z)$ can be found by using the formulas\\n\\n$$\\nx=r\\\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\\\theta ,\\\\,y=r\\\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\\\theta ,\\\\,z=z.\\n$$\\n\\nFirst, let's sketch the projection of $R$ onto the $xy$-plane:\\n\\n![](images/a6/a6b6faca2cd9cb66d463178a24efeb6f47d1dfa0bffe17c657e0d0be2dd11426.png)\\n\\nThe region $R$ is a right vertical cylinder of radius $2$ whose axis passes through the point $(2,0)$ in the $xy$-plane, and is\\n\\n- bounded below by $z=y-4=r\\\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\\\theta -4,$ and\\n\\n- bounded above by $z=3.$\\n\\nWith that in mind, let's write the equation $(x-2)^{2}+{y}^{2}=4$ in polar coordinates:\\n\\n$$\\n\\\\begin{aligned}(x-2)^{2}+{y}^{2} & =4 \\\\\\\\ {(r\\\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\\\theta -2)}^{2}+{(r\\\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\\\theta )}^{2} & =4 \\\\\\\\ {r}^{2}{cos}^{2}⁡\\\\theta -4r\\\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\\\theta +4+{r}^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡\\\\theta  & =4 \\\\\\\\ {r}^{2}({cos}^{2}⁡\\\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\\\theta )-4r\\\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\\\theta  & =0 \\\\\\\\ {r}^{2}-4r\\\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\\\theta  & =0 \\\\\\\\ r(r-4\\\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\\\theta ) & =0\\\\end{aligned}\\n$$\\n\\n \\n\\nSince $r$ cannot be identical to zero on the entire circle, we must have $r=4\\\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\\\theta .$\\n\\nAs a result, in cylindrical polar coordinates, our region can be expressed as \\n\\n$$\\nΔ=\\\\{(r,\\\\theta ,z)\\\\,:\\\\,0\\\\le r\\\\le 4\\\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\\\theta ,\\\\,-\\\\frac{\\\\pi }{2}\\\\le \\\\theta \\\\le \\\\frac{\\\\pi }{2},\\\\,r\\\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\\\theta -4\\\\le z\\\\le 3\\\\}.\\n$$\\n\\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain  \\n\\n$$\\n\\\\begin{aligned}{∭}_{R}z\\\\,\\\\text{d}V & ={∭}_{Δ}zr\\\\,dz\\\\,dr\\\\,d\\\\theta  \\\\\\\\  & ={\\\\int }_{-\\\\pi /2}^{\\\\pi /2}{\\\\int }_{0}^{4\\\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\\\theta }{\\\\int }_{r\\\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\\\theta -4}^{3}rz\\\\,dz\\\\,\\\\text{d}r\\\\,\\\\text{d}\\\\theta .\\\\end{aligned}\\n$$\"} {:db/id 17592186555811 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid \"28bb47d7-b3b3-5032-b1dc-d1d4e63350e0\" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value \"{\\\\int }_{0}^{2\\\\pi }{\\\\int }_{0}^{-4\\\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\\\theta }{\\\\int }_{-r\\\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\\\theta -3}^{3}r({r}^{2}+1)\\\\,dz\\\\,\\\\text{d}r\\\\,\\\\text{d}\\\\theta \" :db/id 17592186555089} {:answer/id #uuid \"3a455c36-c220-5d88-9049-786a2cd87b97\" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value \"{\\\\int }_{\\\\pi }^{2\\\\pi }{\\\\int }_{0}^{-4\\\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\\\theta }{\\\\int }_{-r\\\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\\\theta -3}^{3}r({r}^{2}+1)\\\\,dz\\\\,\\\\text{d}r\\\\,\\\\text{d}\\\\theta \" :db/id 17592186555125} {:answer/id #uuid \"c09e8eb0-74cf-5f58-b58e-f6c62145cf17\" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value \"{\\\\int }_{0}^{2\\\\pi }{\\\\int }_{0}^{-4\\\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\\\theta }{\\\\int }_{-r\\\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\\\theta -3}^{3}r({r}^{2}+1)\\\\,dz\\\\,\\\\text{d}r\\\\,\\\\text{d}\\\\theta \" :db/id 17592186555462} {:answer/id #uuid \"f2223b8c-76e8-51c0-95d5-93e8f8aaa94e\" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value \"{\\\\int }_{\\\\pi }^{2\\\\pi }{\\\\int }_{0}^{-4\\\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\\\theta }{\\\\int }_{-r\\\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\\\theta -3}^{3}{r}^{2}+1\\\\,dz\\\\,\\\\text{d}r\\\\,\\\\text{d}\\\\theta \" :db/id 17592186555596} {:answer/id #uuid \"fc0ff70e-dd05-5b07-bcd7-7e5f6f3c612f\" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value \"{\\\\int }_{\\\\pi }^{2\\\\pi }{\\\\int }_{0}^{-4\\\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\\\theta }{\\\\int }_{-r\\\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\\\theta -3}^{3}{r}^{2}+1\\\\,dz\\\\,\\\\text{d}r\\\\,\\\\text{d}\\\\theta \" :db/id 17592186555628}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid \"3a455c36-c220-5d88-9049-786a2cd87b97\" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value \"{\\\\int }_{\\\\pi }^{2\\\\pi }{\\\\int }_{0}^{-4\\\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\\\theta }{\\\\int }_{-r\\\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\\\theta -3}^{3}r({r}^{2}+1)\\\\,dz\\\\,\\\\text{d}r\\\\,\\\\text{d}\\\\theta \" :db/id 17592186555125} :answer-field/id #uuid \"5b11c795-3f01-5752-821c-85dc64d02b46\" :answer-field/key \"selection\" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186555682}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid \"5d6469f2-3310-5668-93b9-289e45866c22\" :question/math-academy-id \"q-161631\" :question/problem \"If the solid region $R$ is given by \\n\\n$$\\nR=\\\\{(x,y,z)\\\\,:\\\\,{x}^{2}+(y+2)^{2}\\\\le 4,\\\\,-y-3\\\\le z\\\\le 3\\\\},\\n$$\\n\\nthen $$\\n\\\\displaystyle {∭}_{R}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+1\\\\,\\\\text{d}V=\\n$$\" :question/worked-solution \"We will use the change of variables formula in the form\\n\\n$$\\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\\\,dV={∭}_{Δ}\\\\,f(r\\\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\\\theta ,r\\\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\\\theta ,z)\\\\,r\\\\,dz\\\\,dr\\\\,d\\\\theta .\\n$$\\n\\nIf $(r,\\\\theta ,z)$ are the cylindrical polar coordinates of a point, then its Cartesian coordinates $(x,y,z)$ can be found by using the formulas\\n\\n$$\\nx=r\\\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\\\theta ,\\\\,y=r\\\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\\\theta ,\\\\,z=z.\\n$$\\n\\nFirst, let's sketch the projection of $R$ onto the $xy$-plane:\\n\\n![](images/b1/b1886315cede967bc6fd606cdf7bf9d6ae4f82264ccce22211562aa590a8fe4a.png)\\n\\nThe region $R$ is a right vertical cylinder of radius $2$ whose axis passes through the point $(0,-2)$ in the $xy$-plane, and is\\n\\n- bounded below by $z=-y-3=-r\\\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\\\theta -3,$ and\\n\\n- bounded above by $z=3.$\\n\\nWith that in mind, let's write the equation ${x}^{2}+(y+2)^{2}=4$ in polar coordinates:\\n\\n$$\\n\\\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+(y+2)^{2} & =4 \\\\\\\\ {(r\\\\operatorname{cos}\\\\theta )}^{2}+{(r\\\\operatorname{sin}\\\\theta +2)}^{2} & =4 \\\\\\\\ {r}^{2}{\\\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\\\theta +{r}^{2}{\\\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}\\\\theta +4r{\\\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}\\\\theta +4 & =4 \\\\\\\\ {r}^{2}({\\\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\\\theta +{\\\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}\\\\theta )+4r\\\\operatorname{sin}\\\\theta  & =0 \\\\\\\\ {r}^{2}+4r\\\\operatorname{sin}\\\\theta  & =0 \\\\\\\\ r(r+4\\\\operatorname{sin}\\\\theta ) & =0\\\\end{aligned}\\n$$\\n\\n \\n\\nSince $r$ cannot be identical to zero on the entire circle, we must have $r=-4\\\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\\\theta .$\\n\\nAs a result, in cylindrical polar coordinates, our region can be expressed as \\n\\n$$\\nΔ=\\\\{(r,\\\\theta ,z)\\\\,:\\\\,0\\\\le r\\\\le -4\\\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\\\theta ,\\\\,\\\\pi \\\\le \\\\theta \\\\le 2\\\\pi ,\\\\,-r\\\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\\\theta -3\\\\le z\\\\le 3\\\\}.\\n$$\\n\\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain  \\n\\n$$\\n\\\\begin{aligned}{∭}_{R}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+1\\\\hspace{0.222em}\\\\text{d}V & ={∭}_{\\\\mathrm{Δ}}({(r\\\\operatorname{cos}\\\\theta )^{2}+(r\\\\operatorname{sin}\\\\theta )}^{2}+1)\\\\cdot r\\\\hspace{0.222em}\\\\mathrm{d}z\\\\hspace{0.167em}\\\\mathrm{d}r\\\\hspace{0.167em}\\\\mathrm{d}\\\\theta  \\\\\\\\  & ={∭}_{\\\\mathrm{Δ}}({r}^{2}({\\\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\\\theta +{\\\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}\\\\theta )+1)\\\\cdot r\\\\hspace{0.222em}\\\\mathrm{d}z\\\\hspace{0.167em}\\\\mathrm{d}r\\\\hspace{0.167em}\\\\mathrm{d}\\\\theta  \\\\\\\\  & ={\\\\int }_{\\\\bbox[Gainsboro, 2pt]{{\\\\color{blue}\\\\pi }}}^{2\\\\pi }{\\\\int }_{0}^{-4\\\\operatorname{sin}\\\\theta }{\\\\int }_{\\\\bbox[Gainsboro, 2pt]{{\\\\color{blue}-r\\\\operatorname{sin}\\\\theta -3}}}^{3}\\\\bbox[Gainsboro, 2pt]{{\\\\color{blue}r({r}^{2}+1)}}\\\\hspace{0.222em}\\\\mathrm{d}z\\\\hspace{0.222em}\\\\text{d}r\\\\hspace{0.222em}\\\\text{d}\\\\theta .\\\\end{aligned}\\n$$\"} {:db/id 17592186565439 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid \"137dcd13-d6dd-5100-aa1a-4ef83376e70e\" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value \"{\\\\int }_{3\\\\pi /2}^{2\\\\pi }{\\\\int }_{0}^{-4\\\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\\\theta }{\\\\int }_{0}^{r}{r}^{2}z\\\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\\\theta \\\\,dz\\\\,\\\\text{d}r\\\\,\\\\text{d}\\\\theta \" :db/id 17592186564970} {:answer/id #uuid \"6a7ada78-9c1e-53ce-bfd6-73c9704db4ca\" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value \"{\\\\int }_{3\\\\pi /2}^{2\\\\pi }{\\\\int }_{0}^{-4\\\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\\\theta }{\\\\int }_{0}^{r}{r}^{3}z\\\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\\\theta \\\\,dz\\\\,\\\\text{d}r\\\\,\\\\text{d}\\\\theta \" :db/id 17592186565100} {:answer/id #uuid \"8156f8ac-e493-504e-aade-245eb369fa1a\" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value \"{\\\\int }_{\\\\pi }^{2\\\\pi }{\\\\int }_{0}^{4\\\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\\\theta }{\\\\int }_{0}^{r}{r}^{3}z\\\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\\\theta \\\\,dz\\\\,\\\\text{d}r\\\\,\\\\text{d}\\\\theta \" :db/id 17592186565139} {:answer/id #uuid \"9b6bc81a-88bf-5b77-b8c7-6ed75d98d631\" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value \"{\\\\int }_{3\\\\pi /2}^{2\\\\pi }{\\\\int }_{0}^{4\\\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\\\theta }{\\\\int }_{0}^{r}{r}^{2}z\\\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\\\theta \\\\,dz\\\\,\\\\text{d}r\\\\,\\\\text{d}\\\\theta \" :db/id 17592186565174} {:answer/id #uuid \"dd82ed50-ec04-508c-a578-c8dd2bbd72c2\" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value \"{\\\\int }_{\\\\pi }^{2\\\\pi }{\\\\int }_{0}^{-4\\\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\\\theta }{\\\\int }_{0}^{r}{r}^{3}z\\\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\\\theta \\\\,dz\\\\,\\\\text{d}r\\\\,\\\\text{d}\\\\theta \" :db/id 17592186565268}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid \"6a7ada78-9c1e-53ce-bfd6-73c9704db4ca\" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value \"{\\\\int }_{3\\\\pi /2}^{2\\\\pi }{\\\\int }_{0}^{-4\\\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\\\theta }{\\\\int }_{0}^{r}{r}^{3}z\\\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\\\theta \\\\,dz\\\\,\\\\text{d}r\\\\,\\\\text{d}\\\\theta \" :db/id 17592186565100} :answer-field/id #uuid \"c3d4b2ef-ed57-5651-8502-b9160bd5e70c\" :answer-field/key \"selection\" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186565381}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid \"66285a95-784c-5363-bee3-101afc12cafb\" :question/math-academy-id \"q-161635\" :question/problem \"If the solid region $R$ is given by \\n\\n$$\\nR=\\\\{(x,y,z)\\\\,:\\\\,{x}^{2}+(y+2)^{2}\\\\le 4,\\\\,x\\\\ge 0,\\\\,0\\\\le z\\\\le \\\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\\\},\\n$$\\n\\nthen $$\\n\\\\displaystyle {∭}_{R}z({x}^{2}-{y}^{2})\\\\,\\\\text{d}V=\\n$$\" :question/worked-solution \"We will use the change of variables formula in the form\\n\\n$$\\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\\\,dV={∭}_{Δ}\\\\,f(r\\\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\\\theta ,r\\\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\\\theta ,z)\\\\,r\\\\,dz\\\\,dr\\\\,d\\\\theta .\\n$$\\n\\nIf $(r,\\\\theta ,z)$ are the cylindrical polar coordinates of a point, then its Cartesian coordinates $(x,y,z)$ can be found by using the formulas\\n\\n$$\\nx=r\\\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\\\theta ,\\\\,y=r\\\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\\\theta ,\\\\,z=z.\\n$$\\n\\nFirst, let's sketch the projection of $R$ onto the $xy$-plane:\\n\\n![](images/2f/2f36451290eda3db5221e1a5fdbc1647bbedafa97cd47e22d5523d457c4cb83e.png)\\n\\nThe region $R$ is one-half of a right vertical cylinder of radius $2$ whose axis passes through the point $(0,-2)$ in the $xy$-plane, and is\\n\\n- bounded below by $z=0,$ and\\n\\n- bounded above by $z=\\\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}=r.$\\n\\nWith that in mind, let's now write the ${x}^{2}+(y+2)^{2}=4$ in polar coordinates:\\n\\n$$\\n\\\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+(y+2)^{2} & =4 \\\\\\\\ (r\\\\operatorname{cos}\\\\theta )^{2}+(r\\\\operatorname{sin}\\\\theta +2)^{2} & =4 \\\\\\\\ {r}^{2}{\\\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\\\theta +{r}^{2}{\\\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}\\\\theta +4r\\\\operatorname{sin}\\\\theta +4 & =4 \\\\\\\\ {r}^{2}({\\\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\\\theta +{\\\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}\\\\theta )+4r\\\\operatorname{sin}\\\\theta  & =0 \\\\\\\\ {r}^{2}+4r\\\\operatorname{sin}\\\\theta  & =0 \\\\\\\\ r(r+4\\\\operatorname{sin}\\\\theta ) & =0\\\\end{aligned}\\n$$\\n\\n \\n\\nSince $r$ cannot be identical to zero on the entire semicircle, we must have $r=-4\\\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\\\theta .$\\n\\nAs a result, in cylindrical polar coordinates, our region can be expressed as \\n\\n$$\\nΔ=\\\\{(r,\\\\theta ,z)\\\\,:\\\\,0\\\\le r\\\\le -4\\\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\\\theta ,\\\\,\\\\frac{3\\\\pi }{2}\\\\le \\\\theta \\\\le 2\\\\pi ,\\\\,0\\\\le z\\\\le r\\\\}.\\n$$\\n\\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain\\n\\n$$\\n\\\\begin{aligned}{∭}_{R}z({x}^{2}-{y}^{2})\\\\hspace{0.222em}\\\\text{d}V & ={∭}_{\\\\mathrm{Δ}}z({(r\\\\operatorname{cos}\\\\theta )}^{2}-{(r\\\\operatorname{sin}\\\\theta )}^{2})\\\\cdot r\\\\hspace{0.222em}\\\\mathrm{d}z\\\\hspace{0.167em}\\\\mathrm{d}r\\\\hspace{0.167em}\\\\mathrm{d}\\\\theta  \\\\\\\\  & ={∭}_{\\\\mathrm{Δ}}{r}^{3}z({\\\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\\\theta -{\\\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}\\\\theta )\\\\hspace{0.222em}\\\\mathrm{d}z\\\\hspace{0.167em}\\\\mathrm{d}r\\\\hspace{0.167em}\\\\mathrm{d}\\\\theta  \\\\\\\\  & ={\\\\int }_{\\\\bbox[Gainsboro, 2pt]{{\\\\color{blue}3\\\\pi /2}}}^{2\\\\pi }{\\\\int }_{0}^{\\\\bbox[Gainsboro, 2pt]{{\\\\color{blue}-4\\\\operatorname{sin}\\\\theta }}}{\\\\int }_{0}^{r}\\\\bbox[Gainsboro, 2pt]{{\\\\color{blue}{r}^{3}z\\\\operatorname{cos}2\\\\theta }}\\\\hspace{0.222em}\\\\mathrm{d}z\\\\hspace{0.222em}\\\\text{d}r\\\\hspace{0.222em}\\\\text{d}\\\\theta .\\\\end{aligned}\\n$$\"} {:db/id 17592186600830 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid \"08c0fc71-469e-590c-ba8b-284e07e9d56f\" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value \"{\\\\int }_{\\\\pi }^{2\\\\pi }{\\\\int }_{0}^{-2\\\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\\\theta }{\\\\int }_{r\\\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\\\theta }^{{r}^{2}+1}{r}^{3}\\\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\\\theta \\\\,dz\\\\,\\\\text{d}r\\\\,\\\\text{d}\\\\theta\" :db/id 17592186600512} {:answer/id #uuid \"49003dc7-d1f6-5706-9ec6-4e81c0fa6b71\" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value \"{\\\\int }_{\\\\pi }^{3\\\\pi /2}{\\\\int }_{0}^{-2\\\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\\\theta }{\\\\int }_{r\\\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\\\theta }^{{r}^{2}+1}{r}^{2}\\\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\\\theta \\\\,dz\\\\,\\\\text{d}r\\\\,\\\\text{d}\\\\theta\" :db/id 17592186600563} {:answer/id #uuid \"4ada50cc-a17f-524a-b317-1e1efb616136\" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value \"{\\\\int }_{\\\\pi }^{3\\\\pi /2}{\\\\int }_{0}^{-2\\\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\\\theta }{\\\\int }_{r\\\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\\\theta }^{r+1}{r}^{3}\\\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\\\theta \\\\,dz\\\\,\\\\text{d}r\\\\,\\\\text{d}\\\\theta\" :db/id 17592186600567} {:answer/id #uuid \"c950dfc3-14f5-582e-8ccb-600b02b54ca2\" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value \"{\\\\int }_{\\\\pi }^{2\\\\pi }{\\\\int }_{0}^{-2\\\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\\\theta }{\\\\int }_{r\\\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\\\theta }^{r+1}{r}^{2}\\\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\\\theta \\\\,dz\\\\,\\\\text{d}r\\\\,\\\\text{d}\\\\theta\" :db/id 17592186600660} {:answer/id #uuid \"e49f8e80-604e-5e97-939f-664f1887cc80\" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value \"{\\\\int }_{\\\\pi }^{3\\\\pi /2}{\\\\int }_{0}^{-2\\\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\\\theta }{\\\\int }_{r\\\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\\\theta }^{{r}^{2}+1}{r}^{3}\\\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\\\theta \\\\,dz\\\\,\\\\text{d}r\\\\,\\\\text{d}\\\\theta\" :db/id 17592186600684}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid \"e49f8e80-604e-5e97-939f-664f1887cc80\" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value \"{\\\\int }_{\\\\pi }^{3\\\\pi /2}{\\\\int }_{0}^{-2\\\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\\\theta }{\\\\int }_{r\\\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\\\theta }^{{r}^{2}+1}{r}^{3}\\\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\\\theta \\\\,dz\\\\,\\\\text{d}r\\\\,\\\\text{d}\\\\theta\" :db/id 17592186600684} :answer-field/id #uuid \"69082d16-ac02-5957-841e-63c1d5f5e644\" :answer-field/key \"selection\" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186600738}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid \"ce2ff483-7446-5ef4-9532-e3d923aa91d7\" :question/math-academy-id \"q-161634\" :question/problem \"If the solid region $R$ is given by \\n\\n$$\\nR=\\\\{(x,y,z)\\\\,:\\\\,(x+1)^{2}+{y}^{2}\\\\le 1,\\\\,y\\\\le 0,\\\\,x\\\\le z\\\\le {x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+1\\\\},\\n$$\\n\\nthen $$\\n\\\\displaystyle {∭}_{R}{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}\\\\,\\\\text{d}V=\\n$$\" :question/worked-solution \"We will use the change of variables formula in the form\\n\\n$$\\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\\\,dV={∭}_{Δ}\\\\,f(r\\\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\\\theta ,r\\\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\\\theta ,z)\\\\,r\\\\,dz\\\\,dr\\\\,d\\\\theta .\\n$$\\n\\nIf $(r,\\\\theta ,z)$ are the cylindrical polar coordinates of a point, then its Cartesian coordinates $(x,y,z)$ can be found by using the formulas\\n\\n$$\\nx=r\\\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\\\theta ,\\\\,y=r\\\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\\\theta ,\\\\,z=z.\\n$$\\n\\nFirst, let's sketch the projection of $R$ onto the $xy$-plane:\\n\\n![](images/27/27a82956e22b5b2e2de2c02d9d93fb7b7ce57a2b581b88458964eda465e8bebd.png)\\n\\nThe region $R$ is one-half of a right vertical cylinder of radius $1$ whose axis passes through the point $(-1,0)$ in the $xy$-plane, and is\\n\\n- bounded below by $z=x=r\\\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\\\theta ,$ and\\n\\n- bounded above by $z={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+1={r}^{2}+1.$\\n\\nWith that in mind, let's now write the $(x+1)^{2}+{y}^{2}=1$ in polar coordinates:\\n\\n$$\\n\\\\begin{aligned}(x+1)^{2}+{y}^{2} & =1 \\\\\\\\ (r\\\\operatorname{cos}\\\\theta +1)^{2}+(r\\\\operatorname{sin}\\\\theta )^{2} & =1 \\\\\\\\ {r}^{2}{\\\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\\\theta +2r\\\\operatorname{cos}\\\\theta +1+{r}^{2}{\\\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}\\\\theta  & =1 \\\\\\\\ {r}^{2}({\\\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\\\theta +{\\\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}\\\\theta )+2r\\\\operatorname{cos}\\\\theta  & =0 \\\\\\\\ {r}^{2}+2r\\\\operatorname{cos}\\\\theta  & =0 \\\\\\\\ r(r+2\\\\operatorname{cos}\\\\theta ) & =0\\\\end{aligned}\\n$$\\n\\n \\n\\nSince $r$ cannot be identical to zero on the entire semicircle, we must have $r=-2\\\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\\\theta .$\\n\\nAs a result, in cylindrical polar coordinates, our region can be expressed as \\n\\n$$\\nΔ=\\\\{(r,\\\\theta ,z)\\\\,:\\\\,0\\\\le r\\\\le -2\\\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\\\theta ,\\\\,\\\\pi \\\\le \\\\theta \\\\le \\\\frac{3\\\\pi }{2},\\\\,r\\\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\\\theta \\\\le z\\\\le {r}^{2}+1\\\\}.\\n$$\\n\\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain  \\n\\n$$\\n\\\\begin{aligned}{∭}_{R}{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}\\\\hspace{0.222em}\\\\text{d}V & ={∭}_{\\\\mathrm{Δ}}((r\\\\operatorname{cos}\\\\theta )^{2}-(r\\\\operatorname{sin}\\\\theta )^{2})\\\\hspace{0.222em}r\\\\hspace{0.222em}\\\\mathrm{d}z\\\\hspace{0.167em}\\\\mathrm{d}r\\\\hspace{0.167em}\\\\mathrm{d}\\\\theta  \\\\\\\\  & ={\\\\int }_{\\\\pi }^{\\\\bbox[Gainsboro, 2pt]{{\\\\color{blue}3\\\\pi /2}}}{\\\\int }_{0}^{-2\\\\operatorname{cos}\\\\theta }{\\\\int }_{r\\\\operatorname{cos}\\\\theta }^{\\\\bbox[Gainsboro, 2pt]{{\\\\color{blue}{r}^{2}+1}}}\\\\bbox[Gainsboro, 2pt]{{\\\\color{blue}{r}^{3}\\\\operatorname{cos}2\\\\theta }}\\\\hspace{0.222em}\\\\mathrm{d}z\\\\hspace{0.222em}\\\\text{d}r\\\\hspace{0.222em}\\\\text{d}\\\\theta .\\\\end{aligned}\\n$$\"}] :knowledge-point/title \"Rewriting a Triple Integral Defined Over a Shifted Cylindrical Solid\"}",
  "records": []
}
